      ],
      "comments": [
        { "id": "cmt_crx0n6", "address": "$0400", "text": "Screen row 0 is the status line. InitStatusLine ($544C) fills rows 0-1 from txtStatusLineTemplate ($90F8), 61 chars:\n  \"  PLAYER ONE  0000000  NEXT ZONE 00 KM  \" then \"  NEUTRONIUM STATUS  H\"\nSo the score is the 7 digits at $040E-$0414 (scoreField+1..+7), which is exactly what CheckHighScore ($5544) compares. Colour RAM for the screen is screen+$D400 (see PrintCharAt $883A)." },
        { "id": "cmt_x85y33", "address": "$0050", "text": "Difficulty loop counter. Incremented at $9DA4 when all 42 zones are cleared and clamped to 5 at $9DAD, so the game gets harder five times and then stops getting harder. Also read at $9701 to shorten a timer: $43 = 12 - 2*loopNumber." },
        { "id": "cmt_vv4irv", "address": "$0058", "text": "Current zone, 0-41. Compared with #$2A at $9DA0, which is what fixes the game at 42 zones." },
        { "id": "cmt_u3zbt0", "address": "$0340", "text": "Screen row address tables, low bytes at $0340 and high bytes at $0360, used by ScreenAddrFromRow. They live in the tape buffer and are BUILT AT RUNTIME by sub_87FE, so the loaded image has nothing useful here - reading $0340 through this project shows whatever the decruncher left behind, not the table.\n\n27 entries, $0400 stepping by 40. Entries 25 and 26 point past the screen at $07E8 and $0810." },
        { "id": "cmt_ou88u7", "address": "$0421", "text": "The two digits of \"NEXT ZONE nn KM\" on the status line - offsets 33 and 34 of txtStatusLineTemplate. $9D2C decrements them as you travel ($9D35 DEC $0422, borrowing into $0421); $937B sets and carries them the other way. When they reach zero the zone is complete." },
        { "id": "cmt_iwdy8p", "address": "$043C", "text": "THE NEUTRONIUM STATUS BAR, on screen row 1 from column 21. Always addressed as $043C,X or $043C,Y with a 1-based index, so the first cell is $043D.\n\nWhy that address: InitStatusLine ($544C) copies $3D = 61 bytes from txtStatusLineTemplate ($90F8) into $0400, which is row 0 (40 chars) plus the first 21 of the second panel line at $9120 - so it fills $0400-$043C exactly and the bar starts at the next byte.\n\nEight sites move it and they are the self-modifying ones beryl found: $9C12 and $9EB8 among them have their OPCODE poked by $C023/$C046, so a static read of INC/DEC there tells you the addressing mode and not the direction. $9C0C grows it, $9EB6 shrinks it." },
        { "id": "cmt_wxa6dz", "address": "$043C", "text": "THE NEUTRONIUM BAR - the game main resource, and it is stored NOWHERE ELSE. It lives in screen memory: $043D to $044E, eighteen cells, row 1 columns 21-38, right under the words NEUTRONIUM STATUS. $043C is the base the code indexes 1-based.\n\nThat is why $9FAC fills it by writing #$48 to $043C,X for X = 1..$12, and why $9120 carries exactly eighteen H characters and $915E exactly eighteen colour bytes. Three independent places agree on eighteen.\n\nA cell holds a glyph between $48 and $50 - nine states of the same bar segment in the custom charset - and $20 when empty. DrainNeutronium at $9C0C advances one cell per call and empties it when it passes $50; $4A neutroniumLeft is the index of the cell currently being eaten, so it doubles as how much is left.\n\nStoring a resource in the display rather than in a variable is not a shortcut here, it is the whole design: the bar IS the number, and every routine that changes it does so by reading and writing screen RAM." },
        { "id": "cmt_r7xs0u", "address": "$0400", "text": "THE WHOLE SCREEN LAYOUT, put together from five places that each know one row of it. Screen $0400, colour $D800, 40x25.\n\n  row 0     $0400  status line: \"  PLAYER ONE  0000000  NEXT ZONE 00 KM  \"\n                   score = seven ASCII digits at $040E-$0414\n                   distance = two digits at $0421-$0422\n  row 1     $0428  \"  NEUTRONIUM STATUS  \" then the 18-cell fuel bar at $043D-$044E\n  rows 2-11        sky. The twelve stars live here - starInitRow at $8A1A is 03 04 05 06 07 08 09 02 0B 06 07 08, all inside this band\n  rows 9-10 $0590  messageLine, where BEASTS REMAINING and the zone banner are printed\n  rows 14-19 $0630 $0658 $0680 $06A8 $06D0 $06F8  THE SCROLLING TERRAIN, six rows, shifted one column left by the loop at $8C53 with their colour rows $DA30-$DAF8 alongside\n  rows 20-24 $0720  ground, filled with character $28 in colour 6 by the loop at $8AC6\n\nThe six terrain rows and the five ground rows are why $D016 has CSEL cleared at $8871 - 38 columns opens the side border so a column can be shifted in without showing the seam. The fine scroll is $1F counting 7 down to 0 and the coarse shift happens when it wraps, at $8C4D.\n\nSprites are drawn over all of it and the camel walks on the boundary at row 20.\n\n(Added beside basalt earlier note rather than replacing it.)" },
        { "id": "cmt_qmm2u0", "address": "$0630", "text": "First of the six scrolling terrain rows: $0630 $0658 $0680 $06A8 $06D0 $06F8, rows 14-19, with colour rows $DA30 $DA58 $DA80 $DAA8 $DAD0 $DAF8. The loop at $8C53 copies each one byte left across all 39 columns, both character and colour, once every eight frames when the fine scroll at $1F wraps. New columns are pushed in at the right by EmitTerrainColumn from newColumnChars ($1FF0)." },
        { "id": "cmt_7k4buw", "address": "$0033", "text": "camelMode - the player state machine, complete. ControlCamel reads the joystick only in mode 0; every other mode runs an animation in $94F6 and ignores the stick.\n\n  0  on the ground. Left/right walk, up starts a jump, down starts a duck, fire shoots.\n  1  airborne. jumpVelocity ($39) counts $0F down through $00 to $F0 and is added to objYPos as a SIGNED value, so sixteen steps up and sixteen down. At $F0 the camel lands: voice 3 gets a short triangle thud, mode becomes 5 and jumpVelocity is reset to 5.\n  5  landing squash. jumpVelocity counts 4,3,2,1 and the sprite shape comes from the four bytes at $9565 - CE CF CF CE - so the camel compresses and springs back. At zero the shape returns to $C0 and the mode to 0.\n  2  ducking down. INC the sprite shape until it reaches $D2, then mode 3.\n  3  held down. Stays here until the player releases down, or until objXPos reaches $11, the left wall.\n  4  standing up. DEC the sprite shape back to $CD, then shape $C0, mode 0, and clear objMoveType.\n\nSo the whole animation vocabulary is a contiguous run of sprite blocks: $C0 standing, $C0-$CC the walk cycle stepped by $8C2F, $CD jumping, $CE-$D2 the duck, and $CE/$CF again for the landing squash.\n\nFiring is allowed in modes 0, 1 and 5 and refused in 2, 3 and 4 - you cannot shoot while crouched." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "lay_jogeqk",
      "type": "prg",
      "name": "packed",
      "path": "revenge-of-the-mutant-camels.d64:revenge fixed"
    },
    {
      "id": "lay_5d0hyp",
      "type": "prg",
      "name": "runtime",
      "path": "runtime.prg",
      "regions": [
        { "id": "rgn_zxun2r", "start": "$0801", "end": "$1000", "kind": "bitmap", "name": "activeSpriteBank", "comment": "Sprite blocks $20-$3F, the ones the game swaps. CopyCharSetToRAM/$5007 copies a 2K bank here from $1000, $1800, $4000 or $4800 according to the index the wave descriptor supplies in $02. The bytes sitting here in the runtime image are whichever bank was live when the snapshot was taken.", "view": "sprite" },
        { "id": "rgn_8xzthe", "start": "$1000", "end": "$1800", "kind": "bitmap", "name": "spriteBank0", "comment": "2K sprite bank, 32 blocks of 64 bytes. Rendered: the camel, in walk-cycle frames. One of the sources CopyCharSetToRAM/$5007 copies to $0800-$0FFF.", "view": "sprite" },
        { "id": "rgn_4ho3lw", "start": "$1800", "end": "$1D00", "kind": "bitmap", "name": "spriteBank1", "comment": "Sprite data. Rendered: creatures and ships. Note this bank runs out at $1D00 where the object variables begin, so only about 20 of its 32 blocks hold pictures — a copy of the full 2K from $1800 would drag variables along behind them, into blocks $3A-$3F, which nothing appears to display.", "view": "sprite" },
        { "id": "rgn_fq96nq", "start": "$1D00", "end": "$2000", "kind": "data", "name": "objectAndSpriteVariables", "comment": "Not graphics: 768 bytes of game state, 336 instructions reference it. Type templates, live object slots and the sprite multiplexer arrays. See the comments at $1D00, $1E40 and $1F40. It sits here because the VIC bank is $0000-$3FFF and these are sprite blocks $74-$7F, which the game simply never displays." },
        { "id": "rgn_ksx97h", "start": "$2000", "end": "$2400", "kind": "bitmap", "name": "characterSet", "comment": "The game font, and it is HALF a character set: 128 glyphs at $2000-$23FF, not the 2K the VIC could address from here.\n\n$D018 = $18 at $8AE7 puts the character base at $2000. I rendered it: chunky 8x8 glyphs, A-Z at screen codes $01-$1A, digits at $30-$39, punctuation in $20-$3F. Every text table in the program is screen codes for this font, which is why $52 and $56 turn up mid-word in the messages — they are glyphs this font defines and the standard set does not.\n\nCORRECTED after reading the zone data. I first declared this region 2K, up to $2800. It is not: the zone records use sprite blocks $90-$9F, and those are $90*64 = $2400 to $27FF. Rendering $2400 as sprites gives a walking creature in three frames; rendering it as characters gives nothing. So the upper half of what the VIC would call the character set is sprite data, which is fine because the game never writes a screen code above $7F.", "view": "char:8" },
        { "id": "rgn_qypak3", "start": "$2400", "end": "$4000", "kind": "bitmap", "name": "residentSprites", "comment": "7K of sprite data, blocks $90-$FF, and every zone uses it.\n\nThese are NOT in the bank table at $502F and do not need to be: the VIC bank is $0000-$3FFF, so anything from $0800 to $3FFF is reachable by a sprite pointer directly. That is exactly why the table exists — $4000 and $4800 are OUTSIDE the bank, so a set kept there has to be copied down to $0800-$0FFF first.\n\nHow the split works, read off the zone records. Every one of the 42 zones draws its creatures from blocks $20-$3F, which is the SWAPPED bank at $0800-$0FFF, plus blocks in this range, which are always there. So the resident set is the shared vocabulary — explosions at $EC-$EE appear in all 42 zones — and the swapped bank is what makes zone 30 look different from zone 3.\n\nStarts at $2400 rather than $2800: blocks $90-$9F live in what would otherwise be the top half of the character set. Rendered, they are a walking creature.", "view": "sprite" },
        { "id": "rgn_mg792y", "start": "$4000", "end": "$4800", "kind": "bitmap", "name": "spriteBank2", "comment": "Sprite data, out of VIC reach at $4000, so it is copied to $0800 by $5007 when a wave asks for it. Rendered as hires it comes out dithered #.#. all over, which is the signature of MULTICOLOUR sprite data read one bit per pixel: these are two-bit-per-pixel shapes.", "view": "sprite" },
        { "id": "rgn_j3r7na", "start": "$4800", "end": "$5000", "kind": "bitmap", "name": "spriteBank3", "comment": "Sprite data, out of VIC reach, copied to $0800 by $5007. Rendered: explosion or effect shapes and some letter-shaped sprites.", "view": "sprite" },
        { "id": "rgn_1q30n1", "start": "$502F", "end": "$503B", "kind": "data", "name": "charSetSourceTable", "encoding": "screen" },
        { "id": "rgn_gp0fke", "start": "$511B", "end": "$5143", "kind": "text", "name": "txtBeastsRemaining", "encoding": "screen" },
        { "id": "rgn_zi5nmp", "start": "$5199", "end": "$51C0", "kind": "text", "name": "txtStandByYourBeast", "encoding": "screen" },
        { "id": "rgn_rdanr1", "start": "$51C0", "end": "$51C4", "kind": "text", "name": "txtSuffixTwoA", "encoding": "screen" },
