        { "id": "lbl_44bblm", "address": "$9B94", "name": "AddScoreForType", "type": "function" },
        { "id": "lbl_egvexy", "address": "$9BE9", "name": "ExplodeObject", "type": "function" },
        { "id": "lbl_gci8wx", "address": "$9C0C", "name": "DrainNeutronium", "type": "function" },
        { "id": "lbl_rropm1", "address": "$9C4A", "name": "neutroniumColourRamp", "extent": 7 },
        { "id": "lbl_1ib086", "address": "$9CFB", "name": "ReadZoneByte", "type": "function" },
        { "id": "lbl_edxhzf", "address": "$9CFF", "name": "TickMessageLine", "type": "function" },
        { "id": "lbl_bdawm1", "address": "$9D21", "name": "messageColourCycle", "extent": 8 },
        { "id": "lbl_bw6ayy", "address": "$9D2C", "name": "TickZoneDistance", "type": "function" },
        { "id": "lbl_hfskeg", "address": "$9D59", "name": "ZoneComplete", "type": "code" },
        { "id": "lbl_9euq1q", "address": "$9DBB", "name": "SetSpriteFlagsForType", "type": "function" },
        { "id": "lbl_czyoix", "address": "$9E0A", "name": "EmptyNeutroniumBar", "type": "function" },
        { "id": "lbl_vjcu3e", "address": "$9E6B", "name": "RefillNeutroniumBar", "type": "code" },
        { "id": "lbl_ehnya0", "address": "$9EB6", "name": "StepBarCellDown", "type": "function" },
        { "id": "lbl_ki2o6l", "address": "$9EFD", "name": "AddBonusToScore", "type": "function" },
        { "id": "lbl_ld2rj8", "address": "$9F02", "name": "SetSpeedFromFireDirection", "type": "function" },
        { "id": "lbl_rffm8e", "address": "$9F4C", "name": "CamelDies", "type": "code" },
        { "id": "lbl_jq6jii", "address": "$9F9D", "name": "NewLifeOrGameOver", "type": "code" },
        { "id": "lbl_aczyiz", "address": "$C019", "name": "ResetZoneAndFlag", "type": "function" },
        { "id": "lbl_7sjw3i", "address": "$C023", "name": "SetIndexedWriteMode", "type": "function" },
        { "id": "lbl_igua3k", "address": "$C046", "name": "SetIndexedReadMode", "type": "function" },
        { "id": "lbl_6iukcq", "address": "$C061", "name": "SetSpriteFlagsWrapper", "type": "function" },
        { "id": "lbl_jmus12", "address": "$C065", "name": "LoadHighScoreFile", "type": "function" },
        { "id": "lbl_ei2m3d", "address": "$C082", "name": "SaveHighScoreFileAndRestart", "type": "code" },
        { "id": "lbl_rjtqzf", "address": "$C0B9", "name": "ReinitKernalIoAndIrq", "type": "function" },
        { "id": "lbl_l4x88b", "address": "$C100", "name": "highScoreSaveName", "extent": 18 },
        { "id": "lbl_s31qeh", "address": "$C102", "name": "highScoreLoadName", "extent": 16 }
      ],
      "comments": [
        { "id": "cmt_tmo99k", "address": "$C023", "text": "Self-modifying code: patches OPCODE bytes into seven sites in the $9C00-$9FFF routines, then runs them.\n\n  #$C6 = DEC zp   -> $9C22, $9EEA, $9FBB\n  #$99 = STA abs,Y -> $9E68\n  #$9D = STA abs,X -> $9FB0\n  #$DE = DEC abs,X -> $9EB8\n  #$FE = INC abs,X -> $9C12\n\nEvery one of those is a WRITE or a decrement. $C046 is the exact mirror and pokes LDA in the same seven slots. So the code at $9DBB/$9E0A is one routine driven in two modes, and a static reading of those seven addresses is reading whichever opcode was poked last.\n\nEvidence: byte-for-byte mirror pair, and marking $C046 a function decoded exactly the 10 instructions the byte reading predicted." },
        { "id": "cmt_mgendf", "address": "$C046", "text": "READ-mode half of the pair with $C023: pokes #$A5 (LDA zp) into $9C22/$9EEA/$9FBB, #$B9 (LDA abs,Y) into $9E68, #$BD (LDA abs,X) into $9C12/$9EB8/$9FB0, then falls through into $C061 which calls $9DBB.\n\nWas sitting as data until marked a function; nothing references it absolutely, so it is reached by a computed or table dispatch that find_references cannot see." },
        { "id": "cmt_wtwrjw", "address": "$C000", "text": "CMP #$50 twice in a row, and the second is dead: if the first BEQ did not take, A is unchanged, so the second compare has the same result and its BEQ cannot take either. Reads as a leftover from an edit, probably two different key codes once. Live behaviour: $DC01 == $50 goes to $C012, anything else JMP $94AF." },
        { "id": "cmt_66ozf4", "address": "$C023", "text": "Self-modifying patch: writes bytes into six sites in the sound and sprite code ($9C12, $9C22, $9E68, $9EB8, $9FB0, $9FBB) and then calls sub_9E0A. $C046 just below holds a second, unreferenced set of values for the same six addresses. Called from loc_9D59, which is on the $C000 path." },
        { "id": "cmt_ui5gfb", "address": "$C046", "text": "Not reached by any decode: 27 bytes with exactly the shape of sub_C023 above (LDA #$A5 / STA $9C22 / STA $9EEA / STA $9FBB / LDA #$B9 / STA $9E68 / LDA #$BD / STA $9C12 / STA $9EB8 / STA $9FB0). sub_C023 patches the same six addresses with a different set of values, so this is the other half of a pair - a second mode, or the undo - that nothing calls any more. Dead unless something reaches it through a computed jump." },
        { "id": "cmt_2pcme0", "address": "$C065", "text": "Load the high score table from disk.\nSETLFS 0,8,0 then SETNAM $10 chars at $C102 (\"ATTACK MUTANT.HI\"), then LOAD with A=0 (load) and X/Y = $5E00 as the destination, because secondary address 0 means \"ignore the load address in the file header\". So the table lives at $5E00, and the save at $C082 writes $5E00-$5ECF, which fixes its size at $D0 = 208 bytes.\nEvidence: the filename bytes at $C102, and the $FB/$FC pointer set to $5E00 at $C0A5 with X/Y = $5ED0 at $C0AD before JSR SAVE.\nNote the filename says ATTACK, not REVENGE. Either this build reuses the earlier game score file or the disk is mislabelled. Not resolved here." },
        { "id": "cmt_ro4im2", "address": "$C082", "text": "Game over: write the high score table back to disk, then restart at $8A3C.\nDoes a full KERNAL I/O reset first (IOINIT / RESTOR / CINT, and CINV back to $EA31) because the game runs its own raster IRQ and has taken the machine over; SAVE needs the KERNAL IRQ and screen back. Border and background are blanked to 0 so the restored screen is not seen.\nSaves $5E00-$5ECF under \"@:ATTACK MUTANT.HI\" - the @: prefix is the 1541 scratch-and-replace, so it overwrites rather than failing with FILE EXISTS." },
        { "id": "cmt_yydgw1", "address": "$C0B9", "text": "Put the KERNAL back: IOINIT, RESTOR (which restores the $0314 vector table), CINT (screen editor init), then CINV = $EA31, the standard IRQ. Called only from the save path." },
        { "id": "cmt_zoe5ib", "address": "$886F", "text": "Take the machine over: raster IRQ in, NMI disabled, VIC into multicolour text.\n- CINV = $88D5, the raster handler.\n- NMINV = $88D4, which is a bare RTI. That is the standard way to make RESTORE do nothing.\n- $0291 = $80 disables the Commodore+SHIFT charset toggle.\n- $D01A = $81 enables the raster interrupt source; $D012 = $60 and $D011 bit 7 cleared put the split at raster line $60 (96).\n- $D016: AND #$F7 clears CSEL (38-column, so the side borders open for smooth scrolling) and ORA #$10 sets MCM (multicolour text).\n- $D418 = $0F, full volume.\nAlso seeds the music tempo: $10 = $11 = 7." },
        { "id": "cmt_s8pnyx", "address": "$88D4", "text": "One byte, $40 = RTI, installed as the NMI vector at $0318. Pressing RESTORE therefore does nothing. It is also the byte immediately before the IRQ handler, so the two vectors are $88D4 and $88D5 - deliberate, not a coincidence." },
        { "id": "cmt_8twlhy", "address": "$88D5", "text": "Raster IRQ handler. If $D019 bit 0 is clear this was not the raster, so hand it to the KERNAL at $EA31 (the CIA timer IRQ is still running and still needs servicing). Otherwise bump the frame counter and go to the raster-split code at $8BC9.\nEverything below - $88E4/$88EC/$88F7/$91BA - is the tail of that handler: it finishes by acknowledging $D019 and falling into $EA31 to restore registers and RTI." },
        { "id": "cmt_izo8zu", "address": "$88F7", "text": "Music player, voices 1 and 2, run once per raster interrupt. This and $91BA are three copies of one routine.\nA tune is a stream of (ticks, note) byte pairs. The pointer is advanced by 2 BEFORE the read, which is why StartMusic seeds it two below the data: $13/$14 = $5FFE so the first pair read is at $6000.\nPer voice: decrement the tick counter; when it hits zero, gate the voice off (STY $D404 with Y=0), step the pointer, read ticks into $17/$19/$2D and the note into A.\n  note $F0 - a rest. Leave the voice gated off for this many ticks.\n  note $FF - end of tune. Clears $1A so nothing plays until StartMusic is called again. (Voice 1 has no $FF test, only voices 2 and 3 do, so voice 1 relies on the others to stop the tune.)\n  anything else - index into noteFreqLo/noteFreqHi at $898C/$89CD (65 notes) and gate on with $21 = triangle + gate.\n$5F non-zero suppresses the gate-on in all three voices while leaving the frequency writes, which is how the tune is silenced without losing its place.\n$10/$11 is a tempo divider: the whole player only runs every $11 interrupts." },
        { "id": "cmt_md52g2", "address": "$898C", "text": "Note frequency tables, 65 entries each: low bytes at $898C-$89CC, high bytes at $89CD-$8A0D. Indexed by the note byte in a tune, so a note is 0..64. The high bytes run 02..54, which is the usual five-and-a-bit octave equal-tempered table for a PAL SID." },
        { "id": "cmt_oih2m5", "address": "$8A39", "text": "The three letters T W O. sub_8A42 copies them over offsets 9-11 of the player two status line, turning the copied template PLAYER ONE into PLAYER TWO. Note the loop runs X = 3,2,1 and stops before X = 0, so $8A38 is never read - the string is $8A39, not $8A38." },
        { "id": "cmt_83db0f", "address": "$90E4", "text": "\"OF THE MUTANT CAMELS\", 20 characters. Read by sub_9019 at $907E. The rest of the title is elsewhere - this is only the second line." },
        { "id": "cmt_wr61eq", "address": "$90F8", "text": "The 40-column status line, copied verbatim into both players lines at $5F00 and $5F80 by sub_8A42, which ANDs each byte with #$3F to turn PETSCII upper case into screen codes.\n  \"  PLAYER ONE  0000000  NEXT ZONE 00 KM  \"\nThat fixes the field offsets used everywhere else: score is the seven digits at offset 14, the zone distance is the two digits at offset 33, and the word ONE is at offsets 9-11. sub_8A42 overwrites those three bytes of the SECOND copy with \"TWO\" from $8A39, which is the whole of what makes $5F80 the player two line." },
        { "id": "cmt_l5iizi", "address": "$9120", "text": "The other 40-column panel line: \"  NEUTRONIUM STATUS  HHHHHHHHHHHHHHHHHH \". The 18 H characters are the neutronium bar, and $915E holds exactly 18 colour bytes for them (07 07 07 04 04 04 repeated - yellow/purple in threes), which is what identifies the bar as 18 segments rather than a word." },
        { "id": "cmt_g4tlfk", "address": "$9170", "text": "Start the tune. Returns immediately if $1A says one is already playing.\nSets the three voice pointers two bytes below their data, because the player adds 2 before reading:\n  voice 1  $13/$14 = $5FFE  ->  tune at $6000\n  voice 2  $15/$16 = $631E  ->  tune at $6320\n  voice 3  $2B/$2C = $659E  ->  tune at $65A0\nThe round destinations are what make this readable; the seeds themselves are not addresses of anything.\nADSR: attack/decay $0A on voices 1 and 2, $0C on voice 3, sustain/release 0 on all three - a plucked note with no sustain. Filter off, volume $0F. All three tick counters start at 1 so the first note is fetched on the very next interrupt." },
        { "id": "cmt_02df3d", "address": "$5007", "text": "CORRECTED (basalt): this copies a SPRITE SET, not a character set, and beryl reached the same conclusion independently. I have renamed it CopySpriteSet.\n\nThe caller puts an even index in $02. spriteSetSourceTable ($502F) is two interleaved byte tables read as LDA $502F,X (low) / LDA $5030,X (high), so X = 0,2,4,6,8,10 selects source $1000, $1800, $4000, $4800, $5000, $5800. It copies 2048 bytes to $0800-$0FFF ($05 is stepped until it reaches $10, which is what bounds it).\n\nEvidence it is sprites:\n- 2048 bytes at $0800 is 32 sprites of 64, i.e. VIC sprite pointer values $20-$3F.\n- I rendered $1000 and $1800 through run_decoder as 24x21 monochrome sprites and got recognisable creatures at both. $4000 gives dithered multicolour-looking shapes. Not glyphs.\n- The only write to VMCSB in the whole program is $8AE7 STA #$18, which puts the character base at $2000 and leaves it there; AnimateCharacters ($9206) writes character data to $2110 and $2148, confirming $2000. So $0800 is not the charset.\n- Its caller $9CB5 sets SPMC0, SPMC1, XXPAND and YXPAND in the same breath.\n- No instruction anywhere reads or writes $0800-$0FFF absolutely, which is what you expect of data only the VIC looks at.\n\nNOTE this routine reuses $02-$05 as two pointers (source $02/$03, destination $04/$05), not as the PrintCharAt arguments those bytes hold everywhere else in this block.\n\nStill unexplained: the last two table entries are $5000 and $5800, which is this program code. Either those slots are never selected, or the interleaved lo/hi reading is wrong for them." },
        { "id": "cmt_omvkuz", "address": "$50D9", "text": "\"BEASTS REMAINING: n\" on messageLine ($0590, screen row 10).\nThe digit is written at $05AD as '0' + beastsRemaining ($54). If the sum is still '0' - i.e. no beasts left - it calls ShowBeastsExtinct ($53EC) which overwrites the line with \"YOUR BEASTS ARE EXTINCT, PLAYER ONE\" (\"TWO\" patched in at $05B2 when playerState is 2). So $54 is the life count and this is the between-lives screen." },
        { "id": "cmt_vripm1", "address": "$51E8", "text": "THE OPTIONS SCREEN. Reached from CheckOptionsKey ($52EE), which fires on LSTX $26 = the 'O' key.\n\nDraws, via PrintCharAt ($883A):\n  row 3 col 15  \"OPTIONS\"\n  row 6 col 9   \"WAVE SEQUENCY: NORMAL\"   ($52E8 \"RANDOM\" overwrites col 24 when waveSequencyRandom is set)\n  row 8 col 14  \"PLAYERS: 1\"              ('2' written at col 23 when playerState is non-zero)\n  ShowZoneDistance ($5300) adds row 10 col 8 \"DISTANCE BETWEEN ZONES: nn\"\n\nThen polls LSTX. The four codes it tests are the function keys in the C64 keyboard matrix:\n  $04 = F1  toggle waveSequencyRandom ($59, EOR #$FF)\n  $05 = F3  playerState ($56) = (playerState+1) AND 1  -> 1 or 2 players\n  $06 = F5  zoneDistance ($57) += 2, wrapping $15 back to 7\n  $03 = F7  leave: PLA/PLA to drop the caller's return address, JMP $8AEA\n\nThe PLA/PLA at $52B6 means this never returns to whoever called it - it exits straight into the game at $8AEA." },
        { "id": "cmt_jnhqsr", "address": "$539B", "text": "SWAP PLAYERS. playerState ($56) is a three-value thing, and this is where that shows:\n  0 = one-player game\n  1 = two-player game, player one up\n  2 = two-player game, player two up\n\n$56 = 0 -> save and restore the same slot, so the swap is a no-op.\n$56 = 1 -> becomes 2, then falls into the save-to-slot-1 path.\n$56 = 2 -> becomes 1, saves to slot 2.\n\nA slot is 40 bytes of the status line ($5F00 for player one, $5F80 for player two) plus three bytes of per-player state: $58, $50 and beastsRemaining ($54), kept at $5F30-$5F32 and $5FB0-$5FB2. LoadPlayerStatusLine ($5359) is the other half.\n\nThe options screen toggles $56 between 0 and 1 only; the value 2 is only ever produced here." },
        { "id": "cmt_puk04h", "address": "$545C", "text": "Rebuild highScoreTable ($5E00) from defaultHighScoreTable ($5474) - the fallback when the .HI file did not load.\nFills $5E00-$5EFF with spaces, then copies $D0 = 208 bytes of ASCII masked AND #$3F.\n\nNothing in the analysis reaches this address: it was 27 bytes of unexplained data until I declared it a function (+12 instructions). Its caller is presumably in the loader path around $C065/$8A3C, reached by a route the static walk does not follow." },
        { "id": "cmt_3vrhjj", "address": "$5474", "text": "The built-in high score table: four entries of 52 bytes, rank digit + 7 score digits + name.\n  1  0000000    I LIKE CAMELS REALLY\n  2  0000000    I GOT NOTHING AGAINST SHEEP EITHER\n  3  0000000    LLAMAS ARE LLOVELY\n  4  0000000    2D SPACE INVADERS RULE THE ZONE OK\nStored as uppercase ASCII and masked AND #$3F on the way to the screen." },
        { "id": "cmt_6hb1rj", "address": "$5544", "text": "HIGH SCORE CHECK, run at end of game.\n\nhighScoreTable ($5E00) is 4 entries of $34 = 52 bytes. Entry n starts at $5E00 + n*$34. Byte 0 is the rank digit '1'..'4'; bytes 1..7 are the 7 score digits; the rest is the name text. That layout is confirmed three ways: the $34 stride here, InitDefaultHighScores ($545C) copying $D0 = 4*$34 bytes, and the renumbering loop at $567F which writes '1'..'4' at $5E00, $5E34, $5E68, $5E9C.\n\nThe comparison walks Y = 1..7 comparing scoreField+Y ($040E-$0414, the score on the status line) against ($60),Y for each of the four entries in turn. Higher than an entry -> $556E inserts: the table below is shifted down $34 bytes ($5587 copies backwards until it hits a $FF sentinel), the new score is written in, and name entry begins at $55AE.\n\nThe table is loaded from disk at $C065 and saved back at $C082 (amber's finding); InitDefaultHighScores supplies it when the file is not there." },
        { "id": "cmt_3g98pu", "address": "$5695", "text": "Block until a key is pressed, then return its game character code.\nWaits for LSTX ($C5) to leave $40 (= no key), then translates through keyMatrixCodeTable/keyResultTable. An unrecognised key is ignored and the wait restarts. Returns $00 for INS/DEL (backspace) and $FF for RETURN." },
        { "id": "cmt_q7yj88", "address": "$56AE", "text": "KEY DECODE TABLES, and the proof that this game uses a CUSTOM CHARACTER SET.\n\nReadKeyMapped ($5695) searches keyMatrixCodeTable ($56CD) for the value in LSTX ($C5) and returns the byte at the same index in keyResultTable ($56AE). Lining the two tables up against the standard C64 keyboard matrix scan codes gives an exact, unambiguous mapping:\n\n  index  result  matrix code  key\n      0    $00        $00     INS/DEL   (handled as backspace at $5645)\n      1    $01        $0A     A\n      2    $02        $1C     B\n    ...   ...        ...      ... straight through to\n     26    $1A        $0C     Z\n     27    $20        $3C     SPACE\n     28    $51        $2C     . (full stop)\n     29    $52        $2F     , (comma)\n     30    $FF        $01     RETURN     (terminates name entry, $5659)\n\nEvery one of the 26 letters lines up. That pins two glyphs of the game's own charset:\n  $51 = '.'   $52 = ','\nwhich are NOT the standard screen codes ($2E and $2C). Two more follow from context in the message text:\n  $54 = '!'   ($57A7 \"WELL DONE! ... CAMELKIND!\")\n  $56 = ':'   ($52C9 \"WAVE SEQUENCY: NORMAL\", $52DE \"PLAYERS: 1\", $533F \"DISTANCE BETWEEN ZONES: 00\")\n\nSo the built-in screen/petscii decoders are correct for A-Z and space in this project and WRONG for punctuation. The charset itself is one of the blocks CopyCharSetToRAM ($5007) moves to $0800." },
        { "id": "cmt_6bnf64", "address": "$57D1", "text": "Draws \"CHEAT MODE OPERATIVE\" on messageLine when cheatFlag ($5E) is set, and sets $4F = 7.\nLike the other ASCII-stored strings in this block ($5474, $57EF, $5848, $90F8) the text is held as plain uppercase ASCII/PETSCII and converted to screen codes at draw time with AND #$3F. The screen-code strings ($511B, $5199, $52C2...) are stored already converted. Both kinds are in this one block, which is worth knowing before declaring an encoding." },
        { "id": "cmt_2r2tyl", "address": "$5817", "text": "PAUSE. Any key down (LSTX not $40) puts \"PAUSE\" on messageLine and holds until the key is released and another is pressed. Returns immediately when cheatFlag ($5E) is set, so cheat mode disables pause." },
        { "id": "cmt_ehy44q", "address": "$9CB5", "text": "basalt: note from outside this block, because it explains something in mine.\n\nThis reads a stream of parameter bytes through sub_9CFB (LDA ($3E),Y / INY) and distributes them: $53, $52, $51, $42, then $02, then SPMC0, SPMC1, one more byte, then 40 characters into messageLine-1 ($058F), then JSR CopyCharSetToRAM ($5007).\n\nSo ($3E) points at a per-zone descriptor: sprite multicolours, a 40-character banner, and a character set index. CopyCharSetToRAM takes that index in $02 and moves 2K from charSetSourceTable ($502F) into $0800-$0FFF. The six slots in that table are $1000, $1800, $4000, $4800, $5000, $5800 - the last two of which are program code, so either they are never selected or my reading of the interleaved lo/hi table is wrong. Whoever owns $3E/$3F can settle it by reading the descriptor data." },
        { "id": "cmt_l7vts3", "address": "$1D00", "text": "OBJECT SYSTEM. Two different things live in $1D00-$1FFF and they are indexed differently.\n\nTYPE-INDEXED (Y = 0..7, one entry per creature type in this wave):\n  $1D00 typeSpawnTimer  counts down; at zero sub_9744 spawns one of that type\n  $1DA0-$1E37          the WAVE DESCRIPTOR, 152 bytes = 19 tables of 8, copied\n                       wholesale from the level stream by loc_9699 (CPY #$98)\n\nSLOT-INDEXED (X = object slot, 16 wide):\n  $1D10 objType   which template this slot was made from (sub_9772 $97BF: TYA STA $1D10,X)\n  $1D20..$1D90, $1E40, $1E50, $1E70, $1E80, $1E90, $1FB0\n\nsub_9772 is the spawn: it copies one column of the template block into the matching slot arrays. That mapping is what named these:\n\n  tmplFieldFB0  $1DA8 -> objFieldFB0 $1FB0\n  tmplShape     $1DB0 -> objShape    $1D30  and sprShape $1F70\n  tmplField40   $1DB8 -> objField40  $1D40\n  tmplField50   $1DC0 -> objField50  $1D50, and AND #$3F -> objAnimBase $1D20\n  tmplSpeedX    $1DE0 -> objSpeedX   $1D60  and objBaseSpeedX $1E70\n  tmplSpeedY    $1DE8 -> objSpeedY   $1D70  and objBaseSpeedY $1E80\n  tmplField80   $1DF0 -> objField80  $1D80\n  tmplSpawnLimit$1DF8 -> objSpawnLimit $1D90\n  tmplColour    $1E18 -> sprColour   $1F60\n  tmplFieldE90  $1E20 -> objFieldE90 $1E90\n\n$1DF8 bit 7 means the type is capped: $9764 masks the low nibble and compares it with $4D, refusing the spawn once that many exist.\n\nloc_96B0 seeds $1D00-$1D07 from $1DA0-$1DA7 and then zeroes $1D08-$1D9F, which is the whole slot space, at wave start.\n\nThe field names ending in a bare address are honest: the copy proves they are one field, the meaning is not settled." },
        { "id": "cmt_rcwitl", "address": "$1E40", "text": "objXPos / objYPos, and SLOT 0 IS THE PLAYER.\n\nloc_5049 reads objXPos,X, compares it with objXPos (element 0) and then sets bit 7 of objBaseSpeedX,X when the object is at or beyond the player and clears it when it is not, mirroring the result into objSpeedX,X. loc_506A does the same for Y through objBaseSpeedY / objSpeedY.\n\nThat is homing: bit 7 of the speed byte is its sign, and the creature is turned to face slot 0. It only makes sense if slot 0 is the thing being chased.\n\nCorroboration: ShowBeastsRemaining ($50DE) plants #$C0 into objXPos before drawing the status line, and $95B9 / loc_962E move objXPos+1 and objYPos+1 with INC and DEC, i.e. slot 1 under some other control.\n\nWhat I have NOT established: whether slot 0 is the player ship or the camel being defended. The game is about defending camels, so either reading is live." },
        { "id": "cmt_m8glqd", "address": "$1F40", "text": "SPRITE MULTIPLEXER STATE. Sixteen virtual sprites on eight hardware ones, alternated frame by frame.\n\nloc_925F toggles $30 between 0 and 1 every frame and then, for hardware sprite X = 8..1, renders EITHER virtual sprite X-1 (arrays $1F40-$1F47 etc.) on even frames OR virtual sprite X+7 (arrays $1F48-$1F4F etc.) on odd frames. sub_929D does the first, sub_92CF the second; both write SP0X+2*(X-1) and SP0Y+2*(X-1).\n\nSo every array here is 16 entries: 0-7 are the frame-0 half and 8-15 the frame-1 half.\n\n  $1F40  sprX      X position\n  $1F50  sprY      Y position\n  $1F60  sprColour SPMC1 value; bit 7 means auto-increment it every frame (sub_929D $92B7), i.e. colour-cycle\n  $1F70  sprShape  sprite pointer, written straight to $07F8+n\n  $1F80  sprInUse  0 = free. sub_92F6 allocates (writes $88), sub_9328 frees.\n\n$1F98/$1F99 are the two $D015 enable masks and $1F9C/$1F9D the two $D010 MSB-of-X masks, one per frame parity. sub_92F6 ORs the new sprite's bit into $1F98 when the slot is 0-7 and into $1F99 when it is 8-15 (it tests bit 3 of the slot number).\n\nEvidence: $92CB STA $07F7,X with X=1..8 writes $07F8-$07FF, which pins the screen at $0400 and the sprite pointers where the hardware expects them." },
        { "id": "cmt_8k1my1", "address": "$5007", "text": "beryl: I think this copies a SPRITE bank, not a character set, and here is the evidence — the existing name says charset and I have left it alone rather than rename over somebody.\n\n- The destination is $0800-$0FFF. Sprite pointers live at $07F8-$07FF (proved at $92CB, STA $07F7,X with X=1..8), and $0800/64 = block $20, so $0800-$0FFF is sprite blocks $20-$3F.\n- The character set is at $2000, not $0800. $8AE7 writes #$18 to $D018: screen $0400 (bits 7-4 = %0001), charbase %100 * $800 = $2000. I rendered $2000 and it is a legible chunky font, A-Z at screen codes $01-$1A and digits at $30-$39.\n- I rendered the sources. $1000 draws as a camel in walk frames at 24x21, three bytes per row, 64 bytes per block. $1800 draws as creatures. $4000 draws as a multicolour sprite set. None of them are 8-byte glyphs.\n\nUnresolved: the table at $502F holds SIX pointers - $1000, $1800, $4000, $4800, $5000, $5800 - and the last two are this code and the text below it. Either indices 8 and 10 are never passed, or something loads data over $5000 after this point. The wave descriptor table, wherever it is, is what would settle it." },
        { "id": "cmt_xquor6", "address": "$5870", "text": "ASSEMBLER SOURCE LEFT IN THE SHIPPED BINARY. Not data the game uses - residue from the machine it was built on, saved into the image because this range was never cleared.\n\nIt is stored the way a Commodore assembler that lives inside BASIC stores it: a chain of [link lo][link hi][line number lo][line number hi] text $00. Following the links from $5877 gives line numbers 13880 to 13990 in steps of ten, which is what proves the format rather than a coincidence of printable bytes:\n\n  13880   STA $07F7,X\n  13890   RTS\n  13900  SSET TYA\n  13910   PHA\n  13920   AND #$07\n  13930   SEC\n  13940   TAY\n  13950   INY\n  13960   LDA #$00\n  13970  TANGE ROL A\n  13980   DEY\n  13990   BNE TANGE (the operand runs off the end of the salvaged text)\n\nSSET/TANGE is a bit-mask builder: take a sprite number 0-7, shift a 1 left that many places. STA $07F7,X with X = 1..8 writes $07F8-$07FF, the sprite shape pointers for a screen at $0400 - which is where this game puts the screen. So these eleven lines are a sprite-enable and shape-set routine from the original source, and the author called the mask loop TANGE and its entry SSET.\n\nThe chain breaks at $5906 because the next link points at $3202, outside this block. There are two more fragments at $5F28 and $5FA8." },
        { "id": "cmt_kv3z6v", "address": "$5F28", "text": "More assembler source residue, same [link][line number]text$00 format as $5870. Lines 15150-15200:\n\n  15150   CLC\n  15160   ADC #$C8\n  15170   STA NEXINI\n  15180   LDA NEXINI+1\n  15190   ADC #$00\n\nAdding 200 to a 16-bit pointer the author called NEXINI, and 200 is the size of the on-screen play area in rows or the stride of a table. The plain text just above at $5F26 reads \"   LDA NEX\", the tail of the line before this fragment.\n\nThese bytes are overwritten at runtime: $5F00-$5F27 is the player one status line, so the game writes over the start of this block on the first frame. It survives in the image only because it was in memory when the build was saved." },
        { "id": "cmt_widgxi", "address": "$5FA8", "text": "The third and best fragment of leftover assembler source. Lines 15240-15260:\n\n  15240  MLOOP JSR STARF\n  15250        JSR BULITZ\n  15260        JSR CCAMEL\n\nThat is the game main loop in the author own names: STARF is the starfield, BULITZ the bullets, CCAMEL the camels. Just above, at $5FA6 and $5FB3, are the tails of two more lines: \"  ONZER JM\" and \"NI1 ; READ DATA TABLE\" - so there is a label ONZER (zone-related) and a commented-out READ DATA TABLE.\n\nA caution about using these: they are names from SOME build, not necessarily this one, and the fragments are eight lines out of a program of thousands. They are evidence about intent, not an index. Nothing here has been renamed on their authority." },
        { "id": "cmt_h5vede", "address": "$6700", "text": "ZONE DATA TABLE. 42 records of 200 ($C8) bytes, $6700-$87CF, immediately below the code at $87FE with 46 bytes of slack.\n\nThe arithmetic that identifies it is at $93D8: $3E/$3F is loaded with $6700 and then $C8 is added once per zone, X coming from zoneNumber ($58). $9699 then copies $98 = 152 bytes from ($3E),Y into $1DA0, so 152 of each 200-byte record are used and 48 are slack.\n\nThe record count is fixed by $9DA0: after a zone, $58 is incremented and compared with #$2A, so zones run 0 to 41 - 42 of them, 42 x 200 = 8400 = $20D0 bytes, $6700 + $20D0 = $87D0. The table ends exactly where the data does.\n\nThe 152 bytes are 19 groups of 8: one row per attribute, one column per object type, since the game has 8 object types per zone. The destination labels give the layout - $1DA0 spawn interval, $1DA8, $1DB0 shape, $1DB8, $1DC0, ... $1DE0 speed X, $1DE8 speed Y, $1DF0, $1DF8 spawn limit, $1E00, $1E10, $1E18 colour, $1E20.\n\nSo a zone is not a map: it is a table of what may spawn, how often, how fast, what shape and what colour. That is the whole level design of this game." },
        { "id": "cmt_mmxwbt", "address": "$9699", "text": "Copy this zone template block into the working tables: 152 bytes from ($3E),Y - which StartZone has pointed at $6700 + 200*zoneNumber - into $1DA0 upwards. Every tmpl* array is a slice of this one copy." },
        { "id": "cmt_26fyv0", "address": "$9D59", "text": "ZONE COMPLETE. Silences all three SID voices, waits for every active sprite to walk off screen (the loop at $9D6E pushes each objXPos up until it wraps and then frees the sprite), then advances the zone.\n\n$9D9C-$9DB3 is where the game length is decided: INC zoneNumber, and if it reaches #$2A = 42 the player has finished all 42 zones - show WELL DONE, bump loopNumber ($50), cap it at 5, and set the zone back to 0 so the game loops harder. That is the only place either counter changes, so 42 zones per loop and 6 loops of difficulty is the whole progression.\n\nNote $9DB5: LDX #$F8 / TXS resets the stack pointer before jumping to $937B, so this never returns to whoever called it. Anything reasoning about the call graph through here is wrong." },
        { "id": "cmt_g21f9p", "address": "$9DBB", "text": "Set the VIC per-sprite flag bits for one object type. X is the sprite number, Y the object type.\n\nBuilds 1<<(X AND 7) into printColour ($05) and 1<<Y into $06, then tests $06 against three per-type masks - $53 multicolour, $51 X-expand, $52 Y-expand - and ORs the sprite bit into $D01C, $D01D and $D017 respectively. So the zone data says which object TYPES are multicoloured or expanded, and this turns that into the per-SPRITE register bits at the moment a sprite is allocated.\n\nWorth comparing with the leftover source at $5870, which contains the same idea written differently:\n  SSET TYA / PHA / AND #$07 / SEC / TAY / INY / LDA #$00 / TANGE ROL A / DEY / BNE TANGE\nThat version uses SEC+ROL and this one CLC+ASL, so it is a rewrite rather than the same code - but SSET is what the author called this." },
        { "id": "cmt_6ex1uh", "address": "$56AE", "text": "Two more glyphs, found later in the wave banners at $6700 and the scroll text at $5A00:\n  $53 = apostrophe   (\"HAVEN'T WE MET SOMEWHERE BEFORE\", \"ROCK 'N' ROLLUPS\", \"WELCOME TO 'REVENGE OF THE MUTANT CAMELS'\")\n  $55 = question mark (\"WHAT IS A BRAHM, ANYWAY?\", \"MORE TEA, VICAR?\")\nTogether with $51, $52, $54, $56 from the key tables above, the game's punctuation occupies a contiguous run:\n  $51 .   $52 ,   $53 '   $54 !   $55 ?   $56 :\nwhich is a deliberate layout rather than six coincidences, and is why I trust the two that were read off context rather than off the key matrix." },
        { "id": "cmt_op7s9f", "address": "$5870", "text": "NOT GAME DATA. This is leftover text from the machine the game was written on: 6502 source lines held in BASIC line format - 2-byte link, 2-byte line number, then the text, then $00. The links point at $5878, $5888, $5891, $589E ... which is consistent with this having been resident at these addresses, and the line numbers run 13880, 13890, 13900, ... in steps of 10.\n\nWhat survives here reads:\n   STA $07F7,X / RTS / SSET TYA / PHA / AND #$07 / SEC / TAY / INY / LDA #$00 /\n   TANGE ROL A / DEY / BNE ...\n\nMore of the same is at $5F26-$5FFF, underneath the player status save slots, and that fragment carries the author's own routine names:\n   ; CLC / ; ADC #$C8 / ; STA NEXINI / ; LDA NEXINI+1 / ; ADC #$00\n   ONZER JM... / NI1 ; READ DATA TABLE\n   MLOOP JSR STARF / JSR BULITZ / JSR CCAMEL\n\nSo the main loop was called MLOOP and it called STARF, BULITZ and CCAMEL - starfield, bullets and camel. ADC #$C8 next to NEXINI is the same $C8 stride as the wave table at $6700, which is a second, independent confirmation of that record size.\n\nThe game overwrites all of this at runtime: $5E00 becomes the high score table and $5F00/$5F80 the two player status slots. It is residue, not code, and nothing reads it." },
        { "id": "cmt_m3sjnw", "address": "$5900", "text": "INTRO MUSIC, two voices, as (duration, note) byte pairs terminated by $F0 $FF $00 $FF.\n  tuneVoice1 $5900-$594B (38 pairs)\n  tuneVoice2 $5980-$59A7 (20 pairs)\n\nStartTune ($5768) points the players at them: $13/$14 = $58FE and $15/$16 = $597E, i.e. each start address minus 2, so the player pre-increments by 2 before its first read. It also sets tunePlaying ($1A) = 1; ShowWellDone ($5787) spins on that byte until the tune finishes.\n\nThe credits text calls this \"THE INTRODUCTORY MUSIC ... ARRANGED FOR THE 64 BY JAMES LISNEY\". I have not found the player itself, so which byte of a pair is pitch and which is length is inference from the shape (the second of each pair moves over a small range, the first is mostly 1, 2, 4, 6, 8, $0D)." },
        { "id": "cmt_c772pm", "address": "$5A00", "text": "THE ATTRACT-MODE SCROLL TEXT, 1024 bytes of screen codes, $5A00-$5DFF.\n\nBase proved at $8A96: LDA #$00 / STA $5C, LDA #$5A / STA $5D, so scrollTextPtr = $5A00. The scroller at $56EC walks it with ($5C),Y and stops when the high byte reaches $5E ($571B CMP #$5E), which is exactly where the high score table begins - so the text is bounded by $5DFF, and the tail is padded with spaces.\n\nIt reads (with the game charset: $51=. $52=, $53=apostrophe $54=! $55=? $56=:):\n\n  WELCOME TO 'REVENGE OF THE MUTANT CAMELS'. THIS PROGRAM WAS WRITTEN OVER A TWO\n  MONTH PERIOD BY JEFF MINTER. THE INTRODUCTORY MUSIC WAS ARRANGED FOR THE 64 BY\n  JAMES LISNEY. THE GAME CONSISTS OF FORTY TWO DIFFERENT ATTACK WAVES. TWO MODES\n  OF PLAY ARE AVAILABLE: NORMAL WAVE SEQUENCY, STARTING AT WAVE 1 AND PROCEEDING\n  TO LEVEL 42, OR RANDOM WAVE SEQUENCY, WHERE YOUR NEXT WAVE IS RANDOMLY DRAWN\n  FROM ANY OF THE FIRST 32. PRESS 'O' AND USE THE FKEYS TO CONTROL THE OPTIONS.\n  ... SPECIAL THANKS ... UNLESS YOU COPIED IT, IN WHICH CASE MAY THE FLEAS OF\n  1000 CAMELS INFEST YOUR ARMPITS FOREVER.\n\nThat text is a primary source for the rest of the project: 42 waves (the wave table at $6700 has exactly 42 records), the two wave-sequency modes (waveSequencyRandom, $59), the random mode drawing from the first 32, and 'O' opening the options screen (CheckOptionsKey at $52EE tests LSTX $26, which is the O key)." },
        { "id": "cmt_tb054b", "address": "$6700", "text": "THE ATTACK WAVE TABLE. 42 records of 200 ($C8) bytes: $6700 to $87CF. This is the single largest thing in the program and nothing referred to it absolutely, so it was invisible to find_references.\n\nHow the base and stride were established, at $93AB:\n  93D8  LDA #$67 / STA $3F      zoneDataPtr high = $67\n  93DC  LDA #$00 / STA $3E      zoneDataPtr low  = $00\n  93E0  LDX $58 (zoneNumber); loop: zoneDataPtr += $C8, DEX\nSo record n is at $6700 + n*200 and everything in a wave is read through ($3E),Y by sub_9CFB.\n\nWithin a record, bytes $A0-$C7 are the 40-character banner shown when the wave starts. That offset is fixed: every one of the 42 lands exactly on a record boundary + $A0, which is what makes the whole layout provable rather than guessed. Bytes $00-$9F are the wave parameters, distributed by $9CB5 and its callers (sprite multicolours, expand flags, a character set index into charSetSourceTable, and more I did not chase).\n\n42 is confirmed independently by the game's own credits text at $5A00: \"THE GAME CONSISTS OF FORTY TWO DIFFERENT ATTACK WAVES\". The last record $8708-$87CF ends 46 bytes before code resumes at $87FE.\n\nWaves, in order (zoneNumber 0-41, displayed as 1-42):\n 0 ASSORTED EASY AVIAN ALIENS!!        21 BUT IT'S ONLY 'SPACE INVADERS'..\n 1 RAINDROPS KEEP FALLING ON MY BEAST  22 NO CRUISE IS GOOD NEWS!!\n 2 MANIC MINTER                        23 DON'T CALL US, WE'LL CALL EWE..\n 3 WACKY WHACKERS                      24 WHAT IS A BRAHM, ANYWAY?\n 4 ATTACK OF THE MUTANT MUTANT CAMELS  25 ZAPSTARS II: TOTALLY WEIRD..\n 5 THIS KIOSK IS A NUCLEAR FREE ZONE   26 MORE TEA, VICAR?\n 6 HAVEN'T WE MET SOMEWHERE BEFORE?    27 EGGBERT AND THE BOUNCING BOMBS\n 7 AGGRESSIVE AUSTRALIAN ALPINISTS     28 BEWARE OF THE KITCHEN DEVILS!\n 8 ROCK 'N' ROLLUPS                    29 FAST FOOD IS BAD FOR CAMELS\n 9 TELECOM'S COMEUPPANCE               30 SEVERAL SINISTER SPACE SHIPS\n10 HAVE A GO AT THESE...               31 SIDNEY AND THE PORTALAVS\n11 INKY, PINKY, BLINKY, AND THUD!      32 EGGCENTRICITY II: EWE BETTER DUCK!\n12 FISH 'N' PHOTONS???                 33 THE WACKY WORLD OF WINDSURFING TODAY\n13 THROUGH PASTURES BLUE...            34 BEWARE THE RUBBER KEYS OF DOOM\n14 Z A P S T A R S                     35 IRATAIN ONSLAUGHT\n15 BATTLESCAR GIGANTICA                36 PAC IT IN, ZONE GUNNER!\n16 SILLY SILICON                       37 ATTACK OF THE AWFUL SOFTWARE\n17 JUGGLIN' CHOPTERS                   38 HI FI HASSLES\n18 RADIOACTIVE REMAINS!!               39 ZAPSTARS III: GEOMETRICA\n19 IT'S ME AND EWE, BABE..             40 BEASTLY BONANZA\n20 CAREFUL WITH THAT AXE, EUGENE       41 REVENGE OF THE MUTANT MUTANT CAMELIDS" },
        { "id": "cmt_vrm2yw", "address": "$9206", "text": "basalt, from outside this block. Character animation, driven off frame counter $2F cycling 0-5 through charAnimFrameOrder ($9259) = 00 01 02 03 02 01, a ping-pong.\n\nFrame f gives offsets $0E = f*$20 and $0F = f*$10, then:\n  32 bytes from charAnimFrames32 ($6240 + $0E) -> $2110   = characters $22-$25\n  16 bytes from charAnimFrames16 ($62C0 + $0F) -> $2148   = characters $29-$2A\n\n$2110 and $2148 place the character set at $2000, which agrees with the only write to VMCSB in the program: $8AE7 stores #$18, so screen $0400 and character base $2000. Four frames of each, so $6240-$62BF and $62C0-$62FF." },
        { "id": "cmt_2rt817", "address": "$6700", "text": "Zone 0: \"ASSORTED EASY AVIAN ALIENS..\"\nHeader at +$98: $08 $00 $63 $08 $00 $00 $06 $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_twj5bt", "address": "$67C8", "text": "Zone 1: \"RAINDROPS KEEP FALLING ON MY BEAST\"\nHeader at +$98: $04 $00 $02 $08 $00 $00 $06 $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_rquviy", "address": "$6890", "text": "Zone 2: \"MANIC MINTER\"\nHeader at +$98: $01 $00 $00 $08 $00 $00 $06 $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_9b0x0o", "address": "$6958", "text": "Zone 3: \"WACKY WHACKERS\"\nHeader at +$98: $80 $00 $00 $08 $00 $0b $01 $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_rmqf0b", "address": "$6A20", "text": "Zone 4: \"ATTACK OF THE MUTANT MUTANT CAMELS\"\nHeader at +$98: $80 $00 $00 $08 $00 $0b $01 $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_e1czn8", "address": "$6AE8", "text": "Zone 5: \"THIS KIOSK IS A NUCLEAR FREE ZONE\"\nHeader at +$98: $14 $04 $00 $08 $00 $0b $01 $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_wz8czi", "address": "$6BB0", "text": "Zone 6: \"HAVEN'T WE MET SOMEWHERE BEFORE?\"\nHeader at +$98: $ff $00 $00 $06 $00 $0e $08 $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_e535ie", "address": "$6C78", "text": "Zone 7: \"AGGRESSIVE AUSTRALIAN ALPINISTS\"\nHeader at +$98: $04 $00 $00 $08 $00 $0a $07 $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_ih8tmz", "address": "$6D40", "text": "Zone 8: \"ROCK 'N' ROLLUPS\"\nHeader at +$98: $26 $00 $06 $08 $00 $0c $0a $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_32sgw4", "address": "$6E08", "text": "Zone 9: \"TELECOM'S COMEUPPANCE\"\nHeader at +$98: $a0 $80 $00 $08 $00 $0b $01 $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_ars8be", "address": "$6ED0", "text": "Zone 10: \"HAVE A GO AT THESE!!!\"\nHeader at +$98: $00 $00 $00 $08 $00 $00 $00 $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_cjul1a", "address": "$6F98", "text": "Zone 11: \"INKY, PINKY, BLINKY, AND THUD.\"\nHeader at +$98: $55 $00 $00 $08 $00 $01 $06 $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_7qcw14", "address": "$7060", "text": "Zone 12: \"FISH 'N' PHOTONS???\"\nHeader at +$98: $19 $00 $00 $08 $00 $0a $07 $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_57y1vx", "address": "$7128", "text": "Zone 13: \"THROUGH PASTURES BLUE!!!\"\nHeader at +$98: $0a $00 $00 $08 $00 $0a $07 $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_nhhyq5", "address": "$71F0", "text": "Zone 14: \"Z A P S T A R S\"\nHeader at +$98: $43 $00 $01 $08 $00 $06 $07 $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_5jb222", "address": "$72B8", "text": "Zone 15: \"BATTLESCAR GIGANTICA\"\nHeader at +$98: $50 $00 $00 $08 $00 $00 $06 $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_df8bpw", "address": "$7380", "text": "Zone 16: \"SILLY SILICON\"\nHeader at +$98: $03 $00 $00 $08 $00 $0c $06 $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_tfggvr", "address": "$7448", "text": "Zone 17: \"JUGGLIN' CHOPTERS\"\nHeader at +$98: $01 $00 $00 $08 $00 $00 $06 $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_ksqw4h", "address": "$7510", "text": "Zone 18: \"RADIOACTIVE REMAINS..\"\nHeader at +$98: $01 $00 $02 $08 $00 $00 $06 $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_jxzwxu", "address": "$75D8", "text": "Zone 19: \"IT'S ME AND EWE, BABE!!\"\nHeader at +$98: $01 $00 $00 $08 $00 $00 $06 $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_1s918a", "address": "$76A0", "text": "Zone 20: \"CAREFUL WITH THAT AXE, EUGENE\"\nHeader at +$98: $01 $00 $00 $08 $00 $00 $06 $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_3vtgib", "address": "$7768", "text": "Zone 21: \"BUT IT'S ONLY 'SPACE INVADERS'!!\"\nHeader at +$98: $01 $00 $00 $08 $00 $00 $06 $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_pccpen", "address": "$7830", "text": "Zone 22: \"NO CRUISE IS GOOD NEWS..\"\nHeader at +$98: $43 $00 $00 $08 $00 $0e $02 $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_h5xtns", "address": "$78F8", "text": "Zone 23: \"DON'T CALL US, WE'LL CALL EWE!!\"\nHeader at +$98: $07 $06 $00 $08 $00 $0b $01 $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_xstvi7", "address": "$79C0", "text": "Zone 24: \"WHAT IS A BRAHM, ANYWAY?\"\nHeader at +$98: $01 $00 $00 $08 $00 $0b $01 $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_o6hwf0", "address": "$7A88", "text": "Zone 25: \"ZAPSTARS II: TOTALLY WEIRD!!\"\nHeader at +$98: $03 $00 $00 $08 $00 $07 $02 $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_j6ows1", "address": "$7B50", "text": "Zone 26: \"MORE TEA, VICAR?\"\nHeader at +$98: $01 $00 $00 $08 $02 $07 $02 $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_4z6mle", "address": "$7C18", "text": "Zone 27: \"EGGBERT AND THE BOUNCING BOMBS\"\nHeader at +$98: $01 $00 $00 $08 $02 $07 $02 $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_pzqpue", "address": "$7CE0", "text": "Zone 28: \"BEWARE OF THE KITCHEN DEVILS.\"\nHeader at +$98: $01 $00 $00 $08 $02 $07 $02 $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_lc4y61", "address": "$7DA8", "text": "Zone 29: \"FAST FOOD IS BAD FOR CAMELS\"\nHeader at +$98: $ff $00 $00 $08 $02 $08 $05 $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_z16d1u", "address": "$7E70", "text": "Zone 30: \"SEVERAL SINISTER SPACE SHIPS\"\nHeader at +$98: $ff $00 $00 $08 $04 $0a $0e $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_kaiklb", "address": "$7F38", "text": "Zone 31: \"SIDNEY AND THE PORTALAVS\"\nHeader at +$98: $01 $00 $00 $08 $04 $0a $0e $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_l1xhxj", "address": "$8000", "text": "Zone 32: \"EGGCENTRICITY II: EWE BETTER DUCK.\"\nHeader at +$98: $01 $00 $00 $08 $04 $0a $0e $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_9asn6c", "address": "$80C8", "text": "Zone 33: \"THE WACKY WORLD OF WINDSURFING TODAY\"\nHeader at +$98: $01 $00 $00 $08 $04 $0a $0e $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_yosj6f", "address": "$8190", "text": "Zone 34: \"BEWARE THE RUBBER KEYS OF DOOM\"\nHeader at +$98: $01 $00 $00 $08 $04 $0a $0e $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_6pygln", "address": "$8258", "text": "Zone 35: \"IRATAIN ONSLAUGHT\"\nHeader at +$98: $01 $0c $0c $08 $04 $0a $0e $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_s84esm", "address": "$8320", "text": "Zone 36: \"PAC IT IN, ZONE GUNNER.\"\nHeader at +$98: $e7 $00 $00 $08 $06 $01 $06 $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_xhfz4j", "address": "$83E8", "text": "Zone 37: \"ATTACK OF THE AWFUL SOFTWARE\"\nHeader at +$98: $01 $00 $00 $08 $06 $01 $06 $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_qlkfas", "address": "$84B0", "text": "Zone 38: \"HI FI HASSLES\"\nHeader at +$98: $01 $00 $00 $08 $06 $01 $06 $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_j1xrd1", "address": "$8578", "text": "Zone 39: \"ZAPSTARS III: GEOMETRICA\"\nHeader at +$98: $01 $00 $02 $08 $06 $01 $06 $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_wfbpk2", "address": "$8640", "text": "Zone 40: \"BEASTLY BONANZA\"\nHeader at +$98: $01 $00 $00 $08 $06 $01 $06 $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_qoqgyw", "address": "$8708", "text": "Zone 41: \"REVENGE OF THE MUTANT MUTANT CAMELIDS\"\nHeader at +$98: $01 $00 $00 $08 $06 $01 $06 $00 = multicolour mask, Y-expand mask, X-expand mask, sprite count, sprite-bank index, SPMC0, SPMC1, unused.\nName is 40 screen codes at +$A0." },
        { "id": "cmt_hxj0ki", "address": "$8D1D", "text": "THE RANDOM NUMBER GENERATOR, and it does not read RAM.\n\n  INC $24 / LDX $24 / LDA $A000,X / RTS\n\n$A000-$BFFF in this image is 8192 bytes of ZERO — I checked every page. If this read RAM the generator would return 0 forever and eleven callers would all take the same branch every time.\n\nIt does not read RAM. Nothing in this program writes $00 or $01 (I searched: zero instructions touch the processor port), so the 6510 port keeps its power-on $37 and BASIC ROM is banked in over $A000-$BFFF. This reads a byte of BASIC ROM and calls it a random number.\n\nThat is a well-worn C64 trick and it is why the game needs no seed and no arithmetic: the ROM is a fixed 8K block of high-entropy-looking bytes that is always there. It is also perfectly deterministic — the same $24 gives the same byte on every machine and every run — which is the sort of thing that matters if anyone ever tries to reproduce a wave.\n\nEleven callers: $8CD4, $8CE1, $8D6F, $8DAC, $8EB9, $8F31, $8F3A, $9387, $97EF, $9804, $9858. TitleColourAndSoundStep at $50BD reads the same table directly, taking AND #$03 of it to pick a border colour.\n\nre64 cannot see any of this: the memory model is flat and a project layer supplying $A000 wins over a ROM nobody has loaded. The zeros are real bytes of the runtime image; they are just never what the CPU reads." },
        { "id": "cmt_x3i7yt", "address": "$1F00", "text": "Starfield state, four 13-byte arrays indexed 1..12 (entry 0 unused, because the seed loop runs X = $0C down to 1 and stops before zero):\n  $1F00 column 0-39\n  $1F10 row\n  $1F20 glyph, counts $1F down to $1B\n  $1F30 delay, counts down to zero and then the star is updated\nWritten by $8AEA, stepped by UpdateStarfield at $8FB0, drawn by UpdateStar at $8FD2. The per-star delay is what gives the field parallax." },
        { "id": "cmt_sax7ff", "address": "$8AEA", "text": "Seed the 12 stars, then take a snapshot of the player state.\n\nThe seeds come from three 12-byte tables that are indexed 1-BASED - LDA table-1,X with X = $0C down to 1 - which is why the operands read as $8A0D, $8A19 and $8A25 rather than as the tables themselves. $8A0D really is the last entry of noteFreqHi, so the listing showing noteFreqHi + $0040,X is correct and not a mis-resolution.\n  starInitColumn $8A0E   03 13 21 16 0F 09 25 1B 07 12 20 19   all below $28, so screen columns\n  starInitRow    $8A1A   03 04 05 06 07 08 09 02 0B 06 07 08   all below $19, so screen rows\n  starColour     $8A26   03 04 06 07 09 03 08 05 0A 07 09 03   all below $10, so colours\nThat the three tables fall in those three ranges and nowhere else is what identifies which is which; nothing in the code says so.\nEvery star starts at glyph $1F and colour cycle 1.\n\n$8B05 then copies zoneNumber, loopNumber and beastsRemaining into BOTH players saved-state blocks at $5F30 and $5FB0, which is how a new game gives player two the same starting position as player one." },
        { "id": "cmt_0m9m06", "address": "$8FD2", "text": "THE STARFIELD - STARF in the leftover source at $5FA8, where the main loop reads MLOOP JSR STARF / JSR BULITZ / JSR CCAMEL. All three are now identified: STARF = $8FB0 driving this, BULITZ = $95B9, CCAMEL = $9444.\n\nOne star per call, index in X, 12 stars (1..12). Four parallel arrays: starColumn $1F00, starRow $1F10, starGlyph $1F20, starDelay $1F30.\n\nThe glyph counts DOWN from $1F to $1B, so a star is drawn as one of five characters and dims as it ages. When it reaches $1B the star is blanked with a space, the glyph resets to $1F and the column decreases by one - so a star moves one column left every five updates and twinkles in between. At column $FF it wraps to $27 (column 39, the right edge).\n\nCORRECTION to what I first wrote here: $8A26,X is not a colour, it is the per-star DELAY reload, written back into $1F30,X on every update. $8FB0 decrements $1F30,X each tick and only calls this when it reaches zero, so each star has its own period - 03 04 06 07 09 03 08 05 0A 07 09 03 ticks. That is PARALLAX: twelve stars at nine different speeds. The colour is not per star at all; $8FB5 sets printColour to 1 for the whole field." },
        { "id": "cmt_k3obeo", "address": "$1FF0", "text": "newColumnChars: the six characters of the next terrain column, staged here before EmitTerrainColumn writes them to screen column 39, rows 14-19. $1FF9 is the colour used for all six.\n\nNote this renders as spriteSet1 + $07Fx wherever it is not named: the label at $1800 carries an extent of 2K that reaches up here, and $1D00-$1FFF is game variables rather than sprite data. The extent is one page too generous." },
        { "id": "cmt_wjnr17", "address": "$8D2F", "text": "THE TERRAIN, and this is what the game is scrolling.\n\nThis writes six characters into $0657, $067F, $06A7, $06CF, $06F7, $071F. Those are 40 apart, so they are ONE VERTICAL COLUMN of the screen: $0657-$0400 = 599 = row 14, column 39. The rightmost column, rows 14 to 19. The colour byte goes to the matching $DA57.. cells, which is $D800+599, confirming colour RAM under a $0400 screen.\n\nSo new ground arrives at the right-hand edge one column at a time, which is how a character-scrolled landscape is fed. loc_8A62 primes the rest of it at $8AC6: char #$28 and colour #$06 into $071F,X and $DB1F,X for X = $C8 down to 1, which is rows 20-24 filled solid blue.\n\nThe column is staged in newColumnChars $1FF1-$1FF6 before it is written, so the profile can be built up over several frames." },
        { "id": "cmt_zk8ax0", "address": "$8DF4", "text": "TERRAIN PROFILE TABLES. Two of them, six bytes per frame, one frame per screen column.\n\n  terrainProfileSmall $8DF4  6 frames  = a low hill\n  terrainProfileLarge $8E24  10 frames = a tall one\n\nRead as six-byte rows they draw a hillside, top of the column first:\n\n  20 20 20 20 20 26      . . . . . /\n  20 20 20 20 26 28      . . . . / #\n  20 20 20 26 28 28      . . . / # #\n  20 20 20 27 28 28      . . . \\ # #\n  20 20 20 20 27 28      . . . . \\ #\n  20 20 20 20 20 27      . . . . . \\\n\n$20 is blank, $28 is solid ground, and $26 and $27 are the rising and falling slope glyphs of the game font. Loop over the frames and you get a hill that climbs and comes back down.\n\nloc_8E74 chooses between them on $22 (CMP #$03 takes the large one) and indexes with $23 * 8, then copies six bytes into newColumnChars. $8EB9 seeds $22 from NextRandom AND #$07 ORA #$01, so the landscape is generated from BASIC ROM bytes.\n\nThe last two bytes of each 8-byte row are $00 $00 and are not copied — the loop runs Y = 1..6." },
        { "id": "cmt_ynkgmx", "address": "$8825", "text": "Screen address for printRow, into $48/$49, with Y left holding printColumn so the caller can do STA ($48),Y.\n\nIt does NOT multiply: it reads two tables at $0340 (low) and $0360 (high) in the tape buffer, which is free once the game has taken the machine over. FOUND THE BUILDER: sub_87FE, the very first thing the game does at $8A3C. It walks $0400 upward in steps of 40 for $1B = 27 entries.\n\nTwenty-seven, not twenty-five. Rows 25 and 26 come out as $07E8 and $0810 - past the end of the screen, into the sprite pointers at $07F8 and then into sprite data. Nothing appears to print there, and PrintCharAt only guards the COLUMN, not the row, so a routine that got its row wrong would corrupt the sprite pointers rather than fail visibly. Worth knowing if anything ever looks wrong there.\n\n$8834 is five orphaned bytes, 20 25 88 B1 48 = JSR $8825 / LDA ($48),Y, followed by the RTS at $8839 that PrintCharAt branches to. That is a read-character-at routine whose own entry point was deleted, leaving its body reachable only as PrintCharAt off-screen exit. Harmless, and a clear sign of hand editing." },
        { "id": "cmt_90a7xg", "address": "$883A", "text": "PRINT ONE CHARACTER. printColumn $02, printRow $03, printChar $04, printColour $05. Preserves X and Y.\n\nRefuses silently if the column is $28 or more - BPL on CMP #$28, so it also refuses columns $80-$FF, which is what makes the star code safe when a star wraps past column 0 to $FF.\n\nThe colour address is the screen address plus $D400, which is $0400 + $D400 = $D800. So this one instruction is the proof that the screen is at $0400 and the colour map at $D800.\n\nbasalt asked for this name in chat; it was in my block, so I have put it in." },
        { "id": "cmt_vytmxg", "address": "$8B5F", "text": "Write one object position into the sprite shadow registers, and maintain the X most-significant-bit mask.\n\nX is the object slot. Sprite index is (X AND 7) so slots 0-7 and 8-15 share the eight hardware sprites - this game multiplexes two sets of eight over the raster split. $07 is the X coordinate in character-ish units and is doubled, $08 the Y. They go to sprX $1F40 and sprY $1F50.\n\nThe carry out of ASL is the ninth X bit, and it is folded into sprMsbXMaskFrame0 $1F9C or $1F9D depending on bit 3 of the slot number - one mask per half of the multiplex. Clearing goes through EOR #$FF / AND and setting through ORA, which is why the two halves of this routine look duplicated: they are the same shift with opposite bit operations." },
        { "id": "cmt_m08c7d", "address": "$8BC9", "text": "RASTER PHASE DISPATCH. $1E is not really a frame counter, it is a phase counter 0-1-2, bumped by the IRQ at $88DF, and this picks which of three raster splits has just fired:\n\n  phase 1  -> next raster $D8, and $D016 X-scroll bits set from scrollX ($1F)\n  phase 2  -> next raster $F8, X-scroll bits cleared and $D011 bit 7 cleared\n  phase 0  -> next raster $90; this is the one that does the work\n\nSo the play field scrolls smoothly between rasters $D8 and $F8 and the panel above it does not, which is the classic split-scroll.\n\nThe work phase reads $D01E (sprite/sprite collision) into $44 - reading it clears it, so this is the only place collisions are latched - then runs the key check, the title flash, object movement every other frame ($20), and the horizontal scroll.\n\nSCROLLING: DEC $1F each pass. At 4 it calls AnimateCharacters. When it passes $FF it reloads 7 and shifts whole character columns left by one, the loop at $8C53 copying $0630 to $062F and so on. Fine scroll for seven steps, coarse scroll on the eighth - textbook, and it means the play field is character-mapped, not bitmapped." },
        { "id": "cmt_tg4do4", "address": "$9444", "text": "beryl (this is inside amber·s block — comment only, I have named nothing here):\n\nTHIS IS THE PLAYER. Joystick port 2 at $DC00, active low: bit 0 up, bit 1 down, bit 2 left, bit 3 right, bit 4 fire.\n\n  right ($9472 AND #$08 clear) -> INC objXPos (slot 0), stop at $90\n  left  ($948E AND #$04 clear) -> stop at $11, set objFieldFB0 = $80 and sprShape = #$C0\n  fire+up   ($94C0) -> $39 = $0F, $3A = the right bit, $33 = 1, sprShape = #$CD\n  fire+down ($94D7) -> $33 = 2, sprShape = #$CE, objFieldFB0 = $80\n\nSo objFieldFB0 (and $29 beside it) is which way the player faces, $33 is the action state — 0 on foot, 1 and 2 the two fire moves — $39 counts a jump down and $3A remembers whether it was launched moving right.\n\nsprShape with no index is $1F70, virtual sprite slot 0. That pins slot 0 as the player and closes the loop on the object arrays: sub_95B9 fires by putting a shot at objXPos+$13, objYPos+2 into SLOT 1.\n\nThe shape numbers confirm the memory map. #$C0, #$CD and #$CE are sprite blocks, so their data is at $C0*64 = $3000, $3340 and $3380 — all inside the residentSprites region at $2800-$3FFF, which is addressable by the VIC directly and is why those sprites are never copied anywhere. The shot·s shape #$DA at $93D5 is $3680, same region.\n\n$34 divides the frame rate by $80 for this routine; $46 non-zero jumps to ZoneComplete." },
        { "id": "cmt_mj36vj", "address": "$95B9", "text": "beryl (amber·s block, comment only):\n\nTHE PLAYER·S SHOT — amber·s BULITZ from the leftover source.\n\n$3C counts down from 12, so this runs once every twelve calls. $95CB tests $DC00 bit 4 (fire) and then bits 0-3 for a direction; all four set means the stick is centred and nothing happens. The direction is latched in $3B, and it is only accepted when $33 (the player action) is 0, 1 or 5.\n\nFiring allocates virtual sprite slot 1 through sub_92F6 and places it at objXPos + $13, objYPos + 2 — an offset from the PLAYER, which is slot 0 — then plays a pulse-wave blip on voice 1 and sets $3D.\n\nloc_962E flies it: bit 0 of $3B moves it up two, bit 1 down two, bit 2 left one, bit 3 right one, every frame, in whatever direction the stick was held when it was fired. $9658 kills it at X = $06 or $B0 and at Y = $10, $F8 or $0F.\n\nSo the shot is fired in eight directions and travels in a straight line. Note it moves TWO pixels vertically and ONE horizontally, which makes the diagonals steep rather than 45 degrees." },
        { "id": "cmt_26ipuh", "address": "$5900", "text": "basalt: two-voice jingle, $5900-$594B and $5980-$59A7, in the same (ticks, note) format as the intro tune at $6000 and read by the same player. StartTune ($5768) seeds $13/$14 = $58FE and $15/$16 = $597E - two below each, because the player advances the pointer before reading.\n\nCallers: $55B1 (just before high-score name entry) and $579C (ShowWellDone), which spins on tunePlaying ($1A) until it finishes. So this is the short end-of-game jingle, not the intro.\n\nRenamed from tuneVoice1/tuneVoice2 to shortTuneVoice1/shortTuneVoice2 to keep them apart from the three-voice intro tune." },
        { "id": "cmt_1eerb6", "address": "$5ED0", "text": "48 spaces. The high score table proper is $5E00-$5ECF (4 x $34); this is padding up to the player status save slots at $5F00 and $5F80. The whole $5E00 page is what $C065 loads from disk and $545C fills with defaults, which is why the padding is part of the file." },
        { "id": "cmt_iu4nzx", "address": "$6000", "text": "THE INTRO TUNE, three voices, $6000-$66F5. Same (ticks, note) pair format as the player at $88F7/$91BA reads; a pair whose note byte is $FF ends the tune, $F0 is a rest.\n\nStartMusic ($9170) seeds the three pointers two below the data, so:\n  voice 1  $6000-$622F   560 bytes, terminator pair $6230, $FF padding to $623F\n  voice 2  $6320-$64B1   402 bytes, terminator pair $64B2\n  voice 3  $65A0-$66F3   340 bytes, terminator pair $66F4\n\nThis is the music the credits text calls \"ARRANGED FOR THE 64 BY JAMES LISNEY\". The three streams begin with identical bytes (02 24 02 2B 02 2B 02 2B 01 29 01 28 01 26 01 29), which is what an arrangement in canon looks like.\n\nThe char animation frames at $6240-$62FF sit between voice 1 and voice 2, and $6300-$631F is zero/$FF padding." },
        { "id": "cmt_2uti2a", "address": "$64BA", "text": "A FOURTH MUSIC STREAM THAT NOTHING PLAYS. 216 bytes of the same (ticks, note) pairs, $64BA-$6591, terminator pair at $6592.\n\nIt is unreachable, and this is checkable rather than a guess. Only two routines ever seed a voice pointer - StartTune ($5768) and StartMusic ($9170) - and between them they name exactly five stream starts: $5900, $5980, $6000, $6320, $65A0. find_instructions over $13-$16 and $2B-$2C returns 16 and 6 sites and every one of them is inside those two routines or inside the players themselves. Nothing points here.\n\nNor can voice 2 run into it: voice 2 is stopped by the $FF note at $64B2, seven bytes before this starts. (Voice 1 is the one with no $FF test, and it is at $6000, on the other side of the char animation data.)\n\nSo either it is a discarded arrangement left in the binary, or it is reached by something no static walk can see. It reads as a real melody rather than filler, which makes the first more likely." },
        { "id": "cmt_5wmgws", "address": "$66F8", "text": "Eight more bytes of the leftover assembler source that fills $5870-$58FF, $5F28-$5F7F and $5FA8-$5FFF: \" JUMP\" $00 then a BASIC link $670C. It ends one byte before the zone table starts at $6700, which is presumably why nobody trimmed it." },
        { "id": "cmt_pcqyrn", "address": "$93F7", "text": "THE MAIN LOOP, and the leftover assembler source at $5FA8 names three of its four calls:\n\n  15240  MLOOP JSR STARF     ->  $8FB0 UpdateStarfield\n  15250        JSR BULITZ    ->  $95B9 FireAndMoveBullet\n  15260        JSR CCAMEL    ->  $9444 ControlCamel\n\nThe fourth call here, $96FC, is the spawner and creature update; either it came after CCAMEL in the source and the fragment stops before it, or it was added later. Nothing else in this program is a JMP-to-self loop of JSRs, and the three names line up with what the three routines do, so the identification is as firm as it can be without more source.\n\nIt never exits. Everything that ends a life or a zone leaves through a JMP - ZoneComplete at $9D59 even resets the stack pointer first." },
        { "id": "cmt_z8esoz", "address": "$9444", "text": "CCAMEL - control the camel. Joystick port 2 at $DC00, bits active LOW: $01 up, $02 down, $04 left, $08 right, $10 fire.\n\nThe camel is object slot 0: objXPos, objYPos, objFieldFB0 and sprShape all at index 0. Bit 7 of objFieldFB0 is the facing flag.\n\nRuns on a divider - DEC camelTick ($34), and only acts when it reaches zero, reloading $80. So the camel moves once every 128 ticks of whatever drives the main loop, which is what makes it walk rather than fly.\n\nGround behaviour:\n  right  -> INC objXPos, clamped at $90\n  left   -> face left (objFieldFB0 = $80, shape $C0), refused at objXPos $11\n  up     -> jump: camelMode = 1, jumpVelocity = $0F, jumpHeldRight remembers whether right was also held, shape $CD\n  down   -> camelMode = 2, shape $CE, refused at the left limit\nNeither left nor right, or the right limit reached, falls into $9492 which clears the facing flag and JMPs to $C000 - the keyboard check.\n\ncamelMode ($33) is the state machine: 0 walking, 1 rising, 2 ducking, 5 landing. Non-zero sends this routine to the physics at $94F6 instead of reading the stick, so you cannot steer while airborne except by the direction you were already holding." },
        { "id": "cmt_gn191w", "address": "$94F6", "text": "Jump physics, reached when camelMode is not zero.\n\nMode 1, rising and falling: objYPos changes by jumpVelocity ($39), treated as SIGNED - bit 7 set means add the complement instead of subtracting, which is how the same counter serves both halves of the arc. jumpVelocity is decremented every step, so it counts $0F, $0E ... $00, $FF ... down to $F0, giving a symmetric parabola sixteen steps up and sixteen down. At $F0 the camel has landed: camelMode becomes 5, and voice 3 gets a short $0300 triangle burst with attack/decay $0C - the thud.\n\nIf jumpHeldRight was set the camel keeps drifting right for the whole jump, clamped at $90 like on the ground." },
        { "id": "cmt_oq5au0", "address": "$95B9", "text": "BULITZ - the bullet. There is exactly ONE, object slot 1, and $3B bulletDirection is both the flag that it exists and the direction it travels.\n\nFiring: on a $0C-tick repeat delay ($3C). Requires fire down AND at least one direction down - the CMP #$0F test rejects fire with the stick centred, so this game has no straight-ahead shot, only eight-way. The direction bits are saved whole into $3B and the bullet then moves by them for its whole life; you cannot steer it.\n\nIt also refuses to fire unless camelMode is 0, 1 or 5 - so you can shoot while running, rising or landing, but not while ducking (mode 2).\n\nThe bullet starts at the camel position plus ($13, $02) and moves 2 pixels vertically or 1 horizontally per step, which makes diagonals steeper than 45 degrees.\n\nIt dies at $9677 when it reaches X $06 or $B0, or Y $10, $0F, $F8 or $F9. Two pairs of Y limits because the vertical step is 2 and the counter can pass a limit on either parity - checking one value would let a bullet escape half the time. That is a real detail, not redundancy.\n\nSound: a falling-pitch pulse on voice 1, but only if no tune is playing ($1A) - so in-game the laser and the music share voice 1 and the music wins." },
        { "id": "cmt_c3jh5m", "address": "$9680", "text": "The laser sound decay. Reuses voice1NoteTicks ($17) as the pitch: decrement it, write it straight to $D401 as the frequency high byte, and gate the voice off when it reaches zero. So the shot is a downward sweep from $40 to $00 over 64 calls. $3D is the flag that this is running.\n\nWorth noting because it is why the music player and the shot fight over voice 1: they use the same zero page byte for two different purposes." },
        { "id": "cmt_2qgetu", "address": "$994F", "text": "MOVEMENT HANDLER 1: constant speed, expressed as a COUNTDOWN rather than a rate.\n\nX and Y are handled identically. Take objSpeedX,X; if it is zero do nothing. Otherwise split it into a sign (bit 7) and a magnitude (bits 0-6), decrement the magnitude, and put it back with the sign restored. When the magnitude reaches zero, reload it from objBaseSpeedX,X and move objXPos,X by objField80,X — adding when bit 7 of the reload value is clear, subtracting when it is set.\n\nSo the byte is a delay, not a velocity: a LARGER value means a SLOWER object. objBaseSpeedX / objBaseSpeedY hold the reload and the direction, and objField80 is the step in pixels.\n\nThat is what makes loc_5049 work. It sets or clears bit 7 of objBaseSpeedX,X and objBaseSpeedY,X by comparing this object against slot 0, which is the player — one bit each, and the creature turns to face you." },
        { "id": "cmt_n27a0y", "address": "$99C0", "text": "MOVEMENT HANDLER 2: ballistic, and it reads the SAME fields differently. Worth knowing before naming anything in the object arrays: there is no single meaning for objSpeedX.\n\nHorizontal: the LOW NIBBLE of objSpeedX,X is a step in pixels, applied every frame, added or subtracted on bit 7. No countdown.\n\nVertical: objSpeedY,X is a signed velocity that is DECREMENTED every frame, which is gravity. While it is positive the object rises by that much; once bit 7 is set the object falls, one pixel at a time, up to $FF-value steps.\n\nThe fall stops at objYPos = $E0, the ground line, and then one of three things happens on objField80,X:\n  zero          -> objSpeedY = #$10, a fixed bounce\n  bit 7 set     -> $9A29 mixes sub_9856 with objField80, masks to 7 bits and adds 4, a varying bounce\n  otherwise     -> DEC objField80,X, then LDY objField80,X and JMP sub_9772, which RESPAWNS THIS SLOT as a different template. So objField80 is a countdown of bounces, and when it runs out the thing turns into something else.\n\nThat last branch is the most interesting thing in the object system: a creature can metamorphose on landing." },
        { "id": "cmt_03vgs3", "address": "$98BD", "text": "THE ANIMATION STEPPER, and it names four object fields at once.\n\n  objAnimFirst $1D30  first frame\n  objAnimLast  $1D40  the frame to stop or turn at\n  objAnimMode  $1D50  bit 6 = ping-pong, bit 7 = currently running backwards, low 6 bits = the frame delay\n  objAnimDelay $1D20  the countdown, reloaded from tmplAnimMode AND #$3F\n\nsprShape,X is the live frame — the same byte the multiplexer writes into $07F8+n, so animating an object is literally counting its sprite pointer up.\n\nThree behaviours:\n- objAnimFirst equals objAnimLast: nothing moves, a still sprite.\n- bit 6 clear: INC sprShape until it reaches objAnimLast, then snap back to objAnimFirst. A loop.\n- bit 6 set: INC to objAnimLast, then set bit 7 and DEC back to objAnimFirst, then clear it. A ping-pong.\n\nUpdateOneObject at $9910 is the per-object tick: count objAnimDelay down, animate, move by type, age, draw." },
        { "id": "cmt_w83erx", "address": "$9A39", "text": "OBJECT LIFETIME, and this is the metamorphosis mechanic.\n\nobjLifetime,X counts down every tick. At zero it is reloaded from tmplLifetime,Y and the object CHANGES INTO SOMETHING ELSE: $9A4F reads $1DD8,Y — the ninth template table, which renders here as spriteSet1 + $05D8 only because the label at $1800 carries a 2K extent that reaches this far — and\n\n  $FF        -> sub_9328, free the slot. The thing dies.\n  anything else -> sub_9772 with that as the new type. The thing becomes it.\n\nSo $1DD8 is tmplNextType and the creature templates form a CHAIN: each type says what it turns into and how long it has first. Together with the bounce-countdown respawn at $9A20 that is two independent routes from one creature to another, and it is the mechanic that makes a Minter attack wave keep changing shape while you watch." },
        { "id": "cmt_2kp7zl", "address": "$96D2", "text": "THE CHEAT CODE, and it spells GOATS.\n\ncheatKeySequence ($96F7) is five keyboard matrix scan codes: $1A $26 $0A $16 $0D = G, O, A, T, S. The routine reads them as two overlapping tables, which is why the data looks one byte short of what the code implies:\n\n  CMP cheatKeySequence,X     ($96F7 + progress) - the key already accepted: still held, do nothing\n  CMP cheatKeySequence+1,X   ($96F8 + progress) - the next key wanted: accept it, INC cheatProgress\n  anything else              - cheatProgress = 0, start again\n\ncheatProgress ($5F) runs 0-4 and when it reaches 4 the accumulator still holds 4, so STA cheatFlag ($5E) sets cheat mode to a non-zero value and jumps to $9406. From there ShowCheatModeBanner ($57D1) puts \"CHEAT MODE OPERATIVE\" on the message line and CheckPauseKey ($5817) returns without pausing.\n\nThe letters were decoded against the standard C64 keyboard matrix, the same table that proves the game charset at $56AE: 26=G, 38=O, 10=A, 22=T, 13=S. Five for five, in that order, in a Jeff Minter game about camels.\n\n(basalt, writing inside amber block - say the word and I will leave the naming to you.)" },
        { "id": "cmt_86zxpo", "address": "$9B94", "text": "SCORING. Y is the object type, and the points come from tmplScoreValue at $1E28 - one byte per type, part of the zone record, so the same creature is worth different amounts in different zones.\n\nThe byte is packed: low nibble = which score digit, high nibble = how many times to add one to it. scoreField is $040D and the seven digits are $040E-$0414, so digit 7 is units, 6 is tens, 5 is hundreds. $15 is therefore one hundred, $56 is fifty, $55 is five hundred. Carry is propagated by hand at $9BCD: a digit passing $3A wraps to $30 and the loop moves one place left.\n\nThe score is kept in ASCII digits in screen memory and nowhere else - there is no binary copy anywhere. Same design as the neutronium bar.\n\nIt also fires the hit sound: voice 2 gated on with attack/decay $0A, and $41 set to 4 so $9B6D sweeps it. A score value of 0 or $FF scores nothing and makes no noise, which is how a zone marks a creature as harmless or as scenery." },
        { "id": "cmt_43k1ig", "address": "$9BE9", "text": "Explosion. tmplExplodeLength at $1E30 - again per type, per zone - gives the number of DrainNeutronium calls the explosion runs for, so an explosion COSTS FUEL. Then voice 3 is gated with frequency high $08 for the bang.\n\nIt borrows printColour ($05) as the loop counter and printChar ($04) to save X, which is why this routine appears to touch the print variables without printing anything." },
        { "id": "cmt_57tof9", "address": "$9C0C", "text": "Drain one step of neutronium.\n\nINC the current bar cell; when it reaches $50 the segment is used up, so blank it to $20 and DEC neutroniumLeft. The two instructions that do that, at $9C12 and $9C22, are the ones $C023 and $C046 rewrite - see the comment there.\n\nWhen neutroniumLeft reaches zero the camel is out of fuel, and which of two things happens depends on zoneOverFlag: $0F means this is the deliberate end-of-zone drain, so set it to $FF and let EmptyNeutroniumBar see that and stop; anything else means the player has run out during play, so titleFlashCounter goes to 1 and that is what starts the death sequence.\n\nEvery three drains ($4B) it steps $4C down through neutroniumColourRamp at $9C4A - 00 0C 0E 0C 0A 09 08 - into camelColourTarget ($40), which $9B6D then fades the camel sprite towards. So the camel visibly changes colour as its fuel runs down, and the ramp being read backwards is why the sequence looks reversed in the data." },
        { "id": "cmt_vs3g2y", "address": "$9E0A", "text": "END OF ZONE: empty the bar, then fill it again. This is the bonus sequence.\n\nFirst half ($9E10): zoneOverFlag = $0F, then call DrainNeutronium over and over with a falling siren on voice 1 ($00 down to $C0 in the frequency high byte) until the flag turns $FF, which DrainNeutronium does when the last segment goes. printRow counts how many drains it took - that is the bonus.\n\nSecond half ($9E52 onwards): double that count, then refill the bar from cell 1 upward with a rising siren, using the self-modified instructions at $9E68 and $9EB8.\n\nSo the reward for finishing a zone with fuel left is measured by emptying the bar and counting, which is exactly the kind of thing you do when the bar is the only place the number is kept." },
        { "id": "cmt_8q0uaz", "address": "$C046", "text": "CORRECTION, with evidence: this is unreachable, and READ mode is never entered in this build.\n\n- find_references reports no inbound reference.\n- The two bytes $46 $C0 do not occur ANYWHERE in the 47K image, so no lo/hi pointer table and no pushed address can reach it either. Only arithmetic on a computed address could, and nothing here does that.\n- The loaded image already holds the WRITE opcodes at all seven patched sites: $9C12 is $FE (INC abs,X), $9C22 is $C6 (DEC zp), $9E68 is $99 (STA abs,Y), $9EB8 is $DE (DEC abs,X). So the program starts in WRITE mode.\n\nWhich makes sub_C023 a re-assertion of the state the program already ships in, and this routine the half that would undo it and never runs. Read as a debug or development switch left in - the same shape as the dead CMP #$50 at $C007 that disables the start-at-zone-41 branch just below.\n\nThat matters for anyone reading $9C0C, $9E0A or $9F90: those routines only ever run in WRITE mode, so the opcodes the listing shows are the ones that execute." },
        { "id": "cmt_z6reyc", "address": "$9406", "text": "Clear the 40-character message line ($058F-$05B6) to spaces, then TAIL-JUMP into ShowCheatModeBanner ($57D1) rather than return - so clearing the line immediately puts \"CHEAT MODE OPERATIVE\" back if cheatFlag is set. That is why ShowCheatModeBanner ends by setting messageTimer ($4F) = 7: the banner is a message like any other and re-arms its own countdown.\n\nEight callers, all of them \"I have finished with the message line\"." },
        { "id": "cmt_mg836u", "address": "$9CFF", "text": "The message line countdown, called once per frame from CheckPauseKey ($5817).\n\nmessageTickDivider ($4E) counts $60 frames; on each expiry it ticks TickZoneDistance ($9D2C) and then, if messageTimer ($4F) is non-zero, decrements it and recolours the whole message line from messageColourCycle ($9D21) = black, blue, red, purple, green, cyan, yellow, white - indexed by the timer, so the line cycles through seven colours over about seven seconds. When the timer reaches zero it jumps to ClearMessageLine.\n\nSo every banner in the game - BEASTS REMAINING, STAND BY YOUR BEAST, CHEAT MODE OPERATIVE, the wave name - is written to $0590 and then colour-cycled and wiped by this one routine." },
        { "id": "cmt_ngccdq", "address": "$9D2C", "text": "Count down \"NEXT ZONE nn KM\" on the status line. zoneKmTickDivider ($47) divides by $10; each expiry decrements the ones digit at $0422, borrowing into the tens at $0421 when it passes $2F (below screen-code 0), and when both reach 0 the zone is complete." },
        { "id": "cmt_d8c7f8", "address": "$9E6B", "text": "Refill the bar, second half of the end-of-zone sequence. StepBarCellDown at $9EB6 walks the current cell down from $4F towards $47; when it gets there the cell is full, so it steps to the next one and the camel colour walks back UP the ramp at $9C4A - the inverse of the drain. It stops either at cell $13 (bar full, and $9EEA pulls the index back) or when printRow, the doubled bonus count, runs out.\n\nA rising siren on voice 1 runs alongside, $00 up to $40, which is exactly the falling siren of the drain played backwards." },
        { "id": "cmt_d3hite", "address": "$9EFD", "text": "Add to the score without going through a type. PHA PHA then JMP into the middle of AddScoreForType at $9BCB, which is the point after that routine has pushed Y and X - so the two dummy pushes exist only to balance the two pulls at $9BE4. X is the digit column and Y the number of ones to add.\n\nIts one caller, $9E22, passes X = 6, Y = 3 on every step of the end-of-zone drain. Digit 6 is the tens column, so finishing a zone pays THIRTY POINTS per step of neutronium left. That is the entire end-of-level bonus, and this is where the number lives." },
        { "id": "cmt_wig28v", "address": "$9F02", "text": "Move type 2: give this object the direction the player last fired in. bulletDirection ($3B) holds the raw joystick bits from the shot, and they become speed $81 (negative 1) or $01 in X and Y. Reached from the type dispatch at $9C9F.\n\nSo a zone can declare a creature that chases the direction of your last shot. That is not something a static table could express, which is presumably why it is a code case rather than a data field." },
        { "id": "cmt_uhdgtv", "address": "$9F4C", "text": "THE CAMEL DIES. Reached from $9465 when titleFlashCounter says the neutronium ran out.\n\nThe animation is three loops, each of them DelayXY-paced so it plays at a watchable speed regardless of what the raster is doing:\n  $9F67  shape $CD, objYPos[0] counted DOWN to $80  - the camel rises off the top of the screen\n  $9F7E  shape $9C, objYPos[0] counted UP to $C8    - it comes back down\n  $9F90  objXPos[0] counted down to zero            - it walks off to the left\nEvery sprite but the camel is freed first ($9F53 over slots 1-7).\n\nThen $9F9D: reset camelMode, the jump state and the move type, refill the neutronium bar to eighteen full cells, reset the colour ramp, and DEC beastsRemaining ($54). If any beast is left, show the count, swap players and start the zone again at $93AB. If not, this is game over: check the high score table and JMP $C082, which writes it to disk and restarts the game.\n\nSo a life costs one beast and always restarts the CURRENT zone with a full bar - there is no progress penalty beyond the beast." },
        { "id": "cmt_kuapu4", "address": "$1DA0", "text": "THE CREATURE TEMPLATE BLOCK: nineteen tables of eight, 152 bytes, copied here from the zone record at $6700 + 200*zoneNumber by loc_9699. Eight creature types, nineteen attributes each. Every one of the nineteen is now accounted for except none — all read somewhere:\n\n  $1DA0 tmplSpawnInterval      how often one appears (-> typeSpawnTimer)\n  $1DA8 tmplMoveType           0 static, 1 constant-speed, 2 ballistic; bit 7 spare\n  $1DB0 tmplAnimFirst          first sprite block\n  $1DB8 tmplAnimLast           last sprite block\n  $1DC0 tmplAnimMode           bit6 ping-pong, low 6 the frame delay\n  $1DC8 tmplTypeWhenShot       become this when hit by the shot ($9B00)\n  $1DD0 tmplTypeWhenExploded   become this when it explodes ($9B63)\n  $1DD8 tmplNextType           become this when its lifetime runs out; $FF = die ($9A4F)\n  $1DE0 tmplSpeedX             see the movement handlers - meaning depends on tmplMoveType\n  $1DE8 tmplSpeedY             ditto\n  $1DF0 tmplField80            step size for handler 1, bounce countdown for handler 2\n  $1DF8 tmplSpawnLimit         bit 7 = capped; low nibble = how many may exist at once\n  $1E00 tmplSpawnX             starting X; 0 with mode $10 means random\n  $1E08 tmplSpawnY             starting Y; 0 with mode $10 means random\n  $1E10 tmplSpawnMode          0 fixed, $10 random-if-zero, anything else relative ($985E)\n  $1E18 tmplColour             sprite colour; bit 7 makes it cycle every frame\n  $1E20 tmplLifetime           ticks before tmplNextType takes over\n  $1E28 tmplScoreValue         points for shooting it\n  $1E30 tmplExplodeLength      explosion duration; 0 means it does not explode\n\nFOUR SEPARATE ROUTES from one creature to another — shot, exploded, aged out, and bounced out ($9A20) — all landing in the same sub_9772. That is the whole design: a wave is a directed graph of eight creature types, and the zone record is its adjacency list.\n\nVerified against real data. Wave 0 gives moveType all 1 or 2, animFirst/animLast all in $90-$EE which is sprite data inside residentSprites, nextType all 0-6 or $FF, colour all 0-15 with the odd bit 7. Nineteen columns of plausible values is not something a wrong stride produces." },
        { "id": "cmt_3z2nf9", "address": "$3000", "text": "THE PLAYER, and it is a camel — I drew it.\n\nSprite block $C0 lives at $C0*64 = $3000, and the player-control routine at $94AA writes #$C0 into sprShape (virtual sprite 0) when you press left, #$CD at $94CF for fire+up and #$CE at $94E5 for fire+down. Rendering those blocks gives a quadruped with a long neck and a head — a camel, in a standing frame and three leaping ones at $3340.\n\nThat closes three separate chains at once. The player is virtual sprite slot 0 and object slot 0, which is what loc_5049 compares every creature against when it turns them to face you. The shapes are in $2800-$3FFF, addressable by the VIC without copying, which is why the bank-swap table at $502F never mentions them. And it confirms the whole sprite-multiplexer reading, because sprShape really is the byte that ends up at $07F8." },
        { "id": "cmt_1rqo0i", "address": "$56EC", "text": "TEXT SCROLLED INTO THE TERRAIN. Called from $8CAB once per new landscape column, and this is what puts the credits, the high score table and \"HERE WE GO\" into the scenery rather than onto a text line - which is why none of that text is drawn by PrintCharAt anywhere.\n\nIt fills the seven characters of the new column ($1FF0 newColumnChars) with spaces and then plants one or two characters into it.\n\nattractScrollState ($5A) is a mode byte with a counter in the low nibble:\n  $00        off - $8CA9 skips this routine entirely\n  $01-$0F    attract mode. Column row 3 gets highScoreTable[scrollIndex] and row 5 gets\n             attractScrollText[scrollIndex] through scrollTextPtr, colour 1 (white). scrollIndex\n             ($5B) advances one per column; when it wraps past $FF the pointer high byte steps,\n             and when that reaches $5E - the end of the credits text - attractScrollState is\n             zeroed and the scroll stops.\n  $10-$1F    \"HERE WE GO\" ($5756), one character per column, rows 3 and 5, colour 4. The low\n             nibble indexes the string; INC then AND #$1F means after 16 columns it becomes $00\n             and the routine switches itself off.\n\nSet to $03 at $8AAF when the program starts (attract mode) and to $10 at $8FA6, immediately before ShowStandByYourBeast - so \"HERE WE GO\" scrolls past you as a life begins.\n\nTwo streams share one index: the high score table and the credits text are both walked by scrollIndex, so during attract the landscape carries both at once, on different rows." },
        { "id": "cmt_qpcrw5", "address": "$925F", "text": "COMMIT THE SPRITES. There is a two-bank frame multiplexer here and IT NEVER RUNS IN THIS BUILD. Both halves matter, so take them apart carefully.\n\nThe machinery: $30 toggles 0/1 every call. $D015 gets sprEnableMaskFrame0 masked by $32 ORed with the mask for the current phase, and $D010 the same for the X most-significant bits. Then for X = 8 down to 1 it looks at sprInUse[$1F87+X] - that is slots 8 to 15 - and draws hardware sprite X-1 from bank 0 via $929D or bank 1 via $92CF depending on whether that slot is occupied and which phase it is. Sixteen object slots, eight sprites, alternating frames.\n\nWhy it is inert: nothing ever occupies slots 8-15. SSET marks sprInUse[Y] and every caller passes Y in 0..7 - FindFreeObjectSlot only searches 2 to $42-1 and $42 is 8 in 41 of the 42 zones and 6 in the other. ClearObjectSlots at $8B20 writes $FF into sprInUse[0] and sprInUse[8] and zero everywhere else, and nothing clears or sets $1F89-$1F8F afterwards. So the test at $9283 finds zero for X = 2..8 and takes $929D every time, and $1F88 is $FF which $92CF refuses outright. Bank 1 - the shadow registers at $1F48/$1F58/$1F68/$1F78 - is written by nothing and read by nothing.\n\nSo the game shows eight sprites, always from slots 0-7, with no flicker. The multiplexer is either unfinished or was disabled by narrowing $42 to 8 in every zone. If a zone record ever carried $42 greater than 8 the second bank would light up, which makes that byte the switch.\n\n(This corrects what I first wrote here, which said objects 8-15 flicker at 25Hz. The code says they would; the data says there are none.)\n\n$92CB and $92F2 both write $07F8-$07FF, the sprite shape pointers, which independently confirms the screen is at $0400." },
        { "id": "cmt_fg9i8p", "address": "$92F6", "text": "SSET - and that is the author own name, matched instruction for instruction against the leftover assembler source at $5870.\n\nThe source fragment reads:\n  13880   STA $07F7,X\n  13890   RTS\n  13900  SSET TYA\n  13910   PHA\n  13920   AND #$07\n  13930   SEC\n  13940   TAY\n  13950   INY\n  13960   LDA #$00\n  13970  TANGE ROL A\n  13980   DEY\n  13990   BNE TANGE\n\nAnd the code here is $92F2 STA $07F7,X / $92F5 RTS / $92F6 TYA / PHA / AND #$07 / SEC / TAY / INY / LDA #$00 / $92FF ROL A / DEY / BNE $92FF. Every instruction, in order, including the RTS and the STA of the routine before it. So $92CF is the routine those first two lines end, $92F6 is SSET and $92FF is TANGE.\n\nThat is the only place in this project where a name is taken from the source rather than inferred, and it is safe because the match is exact rather than thematic.\n\nWhat it does: allocate a sprite for object slot Y. Build 1<<(Y AND 7), OR it into sprEnableMaskFrame0 or Frame1 depending on bit 3 of the slot - so slots 0-7 and 8-15 go to different halves of the multiplex - and mark sprInUse[Y] = $88. Slots 8-15 additionally flip their bit in $32, which CommitSprites uses to decide which half is showing this frame." },
        { "id": "cmt_0ofgch", "address": "$9328", "text": "Free the sprite for object slot Y: sprInUse[Y] = 0 and the bit cleared from whichever enable mask SSET put it in. The same shift loop as SSET, so this is SSET written out again with AND/EOR instead of ORA. Nine callers - anything that kills an object comes through here." },
        { "id": "cmt_v7vkn8", "address": "$96FC", "text": "SPAWN TICK, run once per pass of the main loop.\n\nFirst a timer: $43 counts down and reloads with 12 minus twice loopNumber, so the whole spawn system runs FASTER on later loops of the game - that is the only difficulty knob outside the zone data.\n\nThen the pause key check, the camel colour fade, and finally the spawn scan: for each of the eight object types, decrement typeSpawnTimer[type] and when it hits zero call SpawnOneOfType. So the zone template byte tmplSpawnInterval controls how often each creature appears, per type, per zone.\n\nEnds by jumping to $98A9, which runs the per-object update over slots 2-15." },
        { "id": "cmt_cg3sxz", "address": "$6700", "text": "ZONE 0 of 42: \"ASSORTED EASY AVIAN ALIENS··\"\n\n  +152 -> $53  $08\n  +153 -> $52  $00\n  +154 -> $51  $63\n  +155 -> $42  $08   live object slots\n  +156 -> $02  $00   sprite bank index -> $1000\n  +157 -> $D025 sprite multicolour 0 = $00\n  +158 -> $D026 sprite multicolour 1 = $06\n  +159 read and thrown away ($00)\n\n+0..+151 are the eight creature templates; see the comment at $1DA0. +160..+199 is the title above, in screen codes for the font at $2000." },
        { "id": "cmt_lj3d0e", "address": "$67C8", "text": "ZONE 1 of 42: \"RAINDROPS KEEP FALLING ON MY BEAST\"\n\n  +152 -> $53  $04\n  +153 -> $52  $00\n  +154 -> $51  $02\n  +155 -> $42  $08   live object slots\n  +156 -> $02  $00   sprite bank index -> $1000\n  +157 -> $D025 sprite multicolour 0 = $00\n  +158 -> $D026 sprite multicolour 1 = $06\n  +159 read and thrown away ($00)\n\n+0..+151 are the eight creature templates; see the comment at $1DA0. +160..+199 is the title above, in screen codes for the font at $2000." },
