        { "id": "cmt_yoibo1", "address": "$7DA8", "text": "ZONE 29 of 42: \"FAST FOOD IS BAD FOR CAMELS\"\n\n  +152 -> $53  $ff\n  +153 -> $52  $00\n  +154 -> $51  $00\n  +155 -> $42  $08   live object slots\n  +156 -> $02  $02   sprite bank index -> $1800\n  +157 -> $D025 sprite multicolour 0 = $08\n  +158 -> $D026 sprite multicolour 1 = $05\n  +159 read and thrown away ($00)\n\n+0..+151 are the eight creature templates; see the comment at $1DA0. +160..+199 is the title above, in screen codes for the font at $2000." },
        { "id": "cmt_e65xjz", "address": "$7E70", "text": "ZONE 30 of 42: \"SEVERAL SINISTER SPACE SHIPS\"\n\n  +152 -> $53  $ff\n  +153 -> $52  $00\n  +154 -> $51  $00\n  +155 -> $42  $08   live object slots\n  +156 -> $02  $04   sprite bank index -> $4000\n  +157 -> $D025 sprite multicolour 0 = $0a\n  +158 -> $D026 sprite multicolour 1 = $0e\n  +159 read and thrown away ($00)\n\n+0..+151 are the eight creature templates; see the comment at $1DA0. +160..+199 is the title above, in screen codes for the font at $2000." },
        { "id": "cmt_d4hi8r", "address": "$7F38", "text": "ZONE 31 of 42: \"SIDNEY AND THE PORTALAVS\"\n\n  +152 -> $53  $01\n  +153 -> $52  $00\n  +154 -> $51  $00\n  +155 -> $42  $08   live object slots\n  +156 -> $02  $04   sprite bank index -> $4000\n  +157 -> $D025 sprite multicolour 0 = $0a\n  +158 -> $D026 sprite multicolour 1 = $0e\n  +159 read and thrown away ($00)\n\n+0..+151 are the eight creature templates; see the comment at $1DA0. +160..+199 is the title above, in screen codes for the font at $2000." },
        { "id": "cmt_k0gokh", "address": "$8000", "text": "ZONE 32 of 42: \"EGGCENTRICITY II· EWE BETTER DUCK·\"\n\n  +152 -> $53  $01\n  +153 -> $52  $00\n  +154 -> $51  $00\n  +155 -> $42  $08   live object slots\n  +156 -> $02  $04   sprite bank index -> $4000\n  +157 -> $D025 sprite multicolour 0 = $0a\n  +158 -> $D026 sprite multicolour 1 = $0e\n  +159 read and thrown away ($00)\n\n+0..+151 are the eight creature templates; see the comment at $1DA0. +160..+199 is the title above, in screen codes for the font at $2000." },
        { "id": "cmt_gmuuz8", "address": "$80C8", "text": "ZONE 33 of 42: \"THE WACKY WORLD OF WINDSURFING TODAY\"\n\n  +152 -> $53  $01\n  +153 -> $52  $00\n  +154 -> $51  $00\n  +155 -> $42  $08   live object slots\n  +156 -> $02  $04   sprite bank index -> $4000\n  +157 -> $D025 sprite multicolour 0 = $0a\n  +158 -> $D026 sprite multicolour 1 = $0e\n  +159 read and thrown away ($00)\n\n+0..+151 are the eight creature templates; see the comment at $1DA0. +160..+199 is the title above, in screen codes for the font at $2000." },
        { "id": "cmt_hghwz9", "address": "$8190", "text": "ZONE 34 of 42: \"BEWARE THE RUBBER KEYS OF DOOM\"\n\n  +152 -> $53  $01\n  +153 -> $52  $00\n  +154 -> $51  $00\n  +155 -> $42  $08   live object slots\n  +156 -> $02  $04   sprite bank index -> $4000\n  +157 -> $D025 sprite multicolour 0 = $0a\n  +158 -> $D026 sprite multicolour 1 = $0e\n  +159 read and thrown away ($00)\n\n+0..+151 are the eight creature templates; see the comment at $1DA0. +160..+199 is the title above, in screen codes for the font at $2000." },
        { "id": "cmt_153dm7", "address": "$8258", "text": "ZONE 35 of 42: \"IRATAIN ONSLAUGHT\"\n\n  +152 -> $53  $01\n  +153 -> $52  $0c\n  +154 -> $51  $0c\n  +155 -> $42  $08   live object slots\n  +156 -> $02  $04   sprite bank index -> $4000\n  +157 -> $D025 sprite multicolour 0 = $0a\n  +158 -> $D026 sprite multicolour 1 = $0e\n  +159 read and thrown away ($00)\n\n+0..+151 are the eight creature templates; see the comment at $1DA0. +160..+199 is the title above, in screen codes for the font at $2000." },
        { "id": "cmt_wnhjo4", "address": "$8320", "text": "ZONE 36 of 42: \"PAC IT IN· ZONE GUNNER·\"\n\n  +152 -> $53  $e7\n  +153 -> $52  $00\n  +154 -> $51  $00\n  +155 -> $42  $08   live object slots\n  +156 -> $02  $06   sprite bank index -> $4800\n  +157 -> $D025 sprite multicolour 0 = $01\n  +158 -> $D026 sprite multicolour 1 = $06\n  +159 read and thrown away ($00)\n\n+0..+151 are the eight creature templates; see the comment at $1DA0. +160..+199 is the title above, in screen codes for the font at $2000." },
        { "id": "cmt_bax6bi", "address": "$83E8", "text": "ZONE 37 of 42: \"ATTACK OF THE AWFUL SOFTWARE\"\n\n  +152 -> $53  $01\n  +153 -> $52  $00\n  +154 -> $51  $00\n  +155 -> $42  $08   live object slots\n  +156 -> $02  $06   sprite bank index -> $4800\n  +157 -> $D025 sprite multicolour 0 = $01\n  +158 -> $D026 sprite multicolour 1 = $06\n  +159 read and thrown away ($00)\n\n+0..+151 are the eight creature templates; see the comment at $1DA0. +160..+199 is the title above, in screen codes for the font at $2000." },
        { "id": "cmt_i8fw3k", "address": "$84B0", "text": "ZONE 38 of 42: \"HI FI HASSLES\"\n\n  +152 -> $53  $01\n  +153 -> $52  $00\n  +154 -> $51  $00\n  +155 -> $42  $08   live object slots\n  +156 -> $02  $06   sprite bank index -> $4800\n  +157 -> $D025 sprite multicolour 0 = $01\n  +158 -> $D026 sprite multicolour 1 = $06\n  +159 read and thrown away ($00)\n\n+0..+151 are the eight creature templates; see the comment at $1DA0. +160..+199 is the title above, in screen codes for the font at $2000." },
        { "id": "cmt_u30krx", "address": "$8578", "text": "ZONE 39 of 42: \"ZAPSTARS III· GEOMETRICA\"\n\n  +152 -> $53  $01\n  +153 -> $52  $00\n  +154 -> $51  $02\n  +155 -> $42  $08   live object slots\n  +156 -> $02  $06   sprite bank index -> $4800\n  +157 -> $D025 sprite multicolour 0 = $01\n  +158 -> $D026 sprite multicolour 1 = $06\n  +159 read and thrown away ($00)\n\n+0..+151 are the eight creature templates; see the comment at $1DA0. +160..+199 is the title above, in screen codes for the font at $2000." },
        { "id": "cmt_5r3pxr", "address": "$8640", "text": "ZONE 40 of 42: \"BEASTLY BONANZA\"\n\n  +152 -> $53  $01\n  +153 -> $52  $00\n  +154 -> $51  $00\n  +155 -> $42  $08   live object slots\n  +156 -> $02  $06   sprite bank index -> $4800\n  +157 -> $D025 sprite multicolour 0 = $01\n  +158 -> $D026 sprite multicolour 1 = $06\n  +159 read and thrown away ($00)\n\n+0..+151 are the eight creature templates; see the comment at $1DA0. +160..+199 is the title above, in screen codes for the font at $2000." },
        { "id": "cmt_5jq053", "address": "$8708", "text": "ZONE 41 of 42: \"REVENGE OF THE MUTANT MUTANT CAMELIDS\"\n\n  +152 -> $53  $01\n  +153 -> $52  $00\n  +154 -> $51  $00\n  +155 -> $42  $08   live object slots\n  +156 -> $02  $06   sprite bank index -> $4800\n  +157 -> $D025 sprite multicolour 0 = $01\n  +158 -> $D026 sprite multicolour 1 = $06\n  +159 read and thrown away ($00)\n\n+0..+151 are the eight creature templates; see the comment at $1DA0. +160..+199 is the title above, in screen codes for the font at $2000." },
        { "id": "cmt_z79u1i", "address": "$503B", "text": "Move type: run AWAY from the direction the player last fired. It is SetSpeedFromFireDirection ($9F02) called with bulletDirection ($3B) complemented - EOR #$FF - and the original value put back afterwards, so the shared routine is reused rather than duplicated with the signs flipped.\n\nReached by JMP from $9CAA in the move-type dispatch, so it is a sibling of the $9F02 case rather than a caller of it." },
        { "id": "cmt_9kuk9d", "address": "$5049", "text": "HOME TOWARDS THE PLAYER. X is the object slot; slot 0 is the player, which is why every comparison is against the un-indexed element of the same array.\n\n  objXPos,X >= objXPos[0]  ->  set bit 7 of objBaseSpeedX  (move left, towards the player)\n  objXPos,X <  objXPos[0]  ->  clear it                    (move right)\nand the same for Y through objBaseSpeedY. The result is written to both the base speed and the live speed, so it takes effect on the next move without waiting for a respawn.\n\nBit 7 of a speed byte is its sign - the same convention as $9F02, which writes $81 for negative one and $01 for positive - so this is not a flag being set, it is the sign being chosen. Nothing here changes the magnitude, so a homing creature keeps whatever speed its zone gave it and only ever turns." },
        { "id": "cmt_7qe7am", "address": "$502F", "text": "beryl: SIX entries, and only FOUR are reachable. Settled by reading the data rather than the code.\n\n  index 0 -> $1000    zones 0-25\n  index 2 -> $1800    zones 26-29\n  index 4 -> $4000    zones 30-35\n  index 6 -> $4800    zones 36-41\n  index 8 -> $5000    never selected\n  index 10 -> $5800   never selected\n\nThe index comes from offset +156 of the zone record, and I decoded all 42 records: the value is 0, 2, 4 or 6 and never 8 or 10. So the last two pointers are dead entries left in the table, which is a relief — they point at this code and at the text below it.\n\nWorth noting what the four live ones say about the game: the sprite bank changes exactly three times in 42 zones, so the creatures you meet in the last six are drawn from a set nobody sees for the first thirty-six." },
        { "id": "cmt_td8fu4", "address": "$9735", "text": "beryl: FREE SLOT SEARCH, and the bounds are the object model.\n\nLDX #$02, scan sprInUse,X for a zero, stop at CPX $42. $42 comes from offset +155 of the zone record and is 8 in 41 of the 42 zones (6 in zone 6, HAVEN·T WE MET SOMEWHERE BEFORE).\n\nSo creatures occupy virtual sprite slots 2 to 7 — SIX of them at once — because slot 0 is the player camel and slot 1 is its shot. Returning $FF means the wave is full and the spawn is dropped, which is why tmplSpawnInterval is a rate rather than a schedule." },
        { "id": "cmt_kzzj8h", "address": "$9A5F", "text": "Kill an object that has left the play area. X and Y are treated as sign-magnitude - bit 7 is the sign and the low seven bits the value - so the limits are: X below 5 on the positive side or below $33 on the negative side, Y below $10 positive or $70 or more negative. Anything outside runs the type-specific cleanup at $9C51 and frees the sprite.\n\nSign-magnitude rather than two-complement is unusual and it is consistent across the object system: objSpeedX and objSpeedY use the same convention at $994F, where the low seven bits are a countdown and bit 7 the direction." },
        { "id": "cmt_jmltko", "address": "$9A9C", "text": "COLLISION, and there are TWO tests here with different mechanisms - which is the interesting part.\n\nBULLET vs this object ($9AC2-$9B12): pure coordinates. objX - bulletX - 4 must come out negative and, complemented, below $18; same in Y. So a 24x24 box, tested in software for every object every tick.\n\nCAMEL vs this object ($9B1B-$9B6B): the VIC hardware collision register. $44 is $D01E latched once per frame at $8C01 (reading it clears it, so it can only be read in one place), ANDed with this object sprite bit and with bit 0, the camel sprite. Then confirmed by a coordinate box, $20 wide this time.\n\nWHY THE DIFFERENCE, and this is a finding rather than a guess: the bullet test HAS the hardware gate and it is stubbed out. $9AB3 is LDA #$FF / AND #$02 and $9ABC is LDA #$FF / AND printChar - both immediate $FF, so both always pass. Put $A5 $44 in place of $A9 $FF and they become exactly the shape of the camel test at $9B1B: latch AND sprite-bit. Two bytes, twice, and the routine is symmetrical.\n\nThat is a deliberate disable, and the reason is visible elsewhere in the program: the multiplexer at $925F draws objects 8-15 on alternate frames, so a sprite that is not displayed this frame registers no hardware collision. A bullet that misses half the time is not acceptable; a creature that is hit a frame late is. So the bullet was moved to software and the dead gate left in place.\n\nOn a hit: score by type, then the object becomes tmplTypeWhenShot rather than simply dying, and the bullet is freed. On a camel collision: explode for tmplExplodeLength steps of neutronium and become tmplTypeWhenExploded. Nothing in this game is just destroyed - it turns into the next thing." },
        { "id": "cmt_eg2d2o", "address": "$985E", "text": "SPAWN MODE 3: place the new object RELATIVE TO ANOTHER ONE.\n\ntmplSpawnMode is 0 for a fixed position and $10 for random-if-zero. Anything else lands here, and its low nibble minus one is a creature TYPE: scan slots 2 to 15 for a live object of that type, and if one is found put the new object at that object·s position plus tmplSpawnX and tmplSpawnY.\n\nThat is how a creature spawns something at itself — an egg under a bird, a bomb under a ship — without anything having to pass a position.\n\nAND IF THERE IS NO SUCH OBJECT IT DOES PLA PLA RTS at $9878, discarding its own return address and returning to its caller·s caller. That cancels the whole spawn two levels up rather than creating something in the wrong place. Worth knowing before trusting any call graph through here: PlaceNewObject·s caller does not always get control back." },
        { "id": "cmt_wp8mwr", "address": "$98A9", "text": "THE OBJECT UPDATE LOOP: slots 2 to 15, skipping any whose objMoveType is zero, calling UpdateOneObject on the rest.\n\nNote the range. FindFreeObjectSlot only ever allocates in 2 to $42-1, which is 2 to 7 in 41 of the 42 zones, so spawned creatures live in six slots — 0 is the player camel and 1 is its shot. But this loop runs to 15, which is the full sixteen the sprite multiplexer supports, so slots 8-15 are driven by something other than the spawner and appear on the odd-numbered frames." },
        { "id": "cmt_5yalhb", "address": "$87FE", "text": "Build the screen row address tables at $0340/$0360 that ScreenAddrFromRow reads. $0400 stepping by 40, 27 entries. This is the first thing $8A3C does, before anything else, because nothing can print until it exists." },
        { "id": "cmt_qvk2vs", "address": "$8F7C", "text": "THE TITLE SCREEN LOOP. Starfield, INC $D025 so the sprite multicolour cycles, StartMusic, the options key check, and then read the joystick fire button - anything but fire loops back here forever.\n\nStartMusic returns immediately if a tune is already playing, so calling it every pass is how the title music restarts when it ends rather than being started once.\n\nPress fire and $8F92 takes over: silence the tune, build both player status lines, copy the chosen zone distance into the player two line, and start the game at $937B." },
        { "id": "cmt_ymv6pq", "address": "$0801", "text": "HOW THE SPRITE MEMORY IS SPLIT, read off all 42 zone records rather than guessed.\n\nEvery zone draws its creatures from two places:\n\n  blocks $20-$3F   $0800-$0FFF   THE SWAPPED BANK. Contents depend on which 2K CopyCharSetToRAM/$5007 last copied here, chosen per zone: bank $1000 for zones 0-25, $1800 for 26-29, $4000 for 30-35, $4800 for 36-41. Three swaps in 42 zones.\n  blocks $90-$FF   $2400-$3FFF   RESIDENT. Always there, because the VIC bank is $0000-$3FFF and a pointer can reach them without a copy.\n\nThe resident set is the shared vocabulary — the player camel at $C0 and $CD-$D2, the explosion at $EC-$EE which every single zone uses, the shot at $DA. The swapped bank is what makes zone 30 look different from zone 3.\n\nBlocks $40-$8F ($1000-$23FF) are never used as sprite pointers by any zone, which is consistent: $1000-$1FFF is bank source and variables, and $2000-$23FF is the font." },
        { "id": "cmt_90ba2x", "address": "$3B00", "text": "THE EXPLOSION, and it is an ordinary creature.\n\nBlocks $EC, $ED, $EE. Rendered they are three frames of a sparse symmetric dot pattern spreading outward — a starburst.\n\nWhat makes this worth a comment is that every one of the 42 zone records devotes one of its eight CREATURE TYPES to it. Check zone 0: type 3 is animFirst = animLast·s range $EC-$EE, moveType 1, lifetime $0C, tmplNextType $FF. A short-lived object that dies when its time is up. And tmplTypeWhenExploded for the other seven types is 3, 3, 3, 0, 3, 3, 3, 3 — they all turn INTO type 3 when they explode.\n\nSo the game has no explosion subsystem. An explosion is a creature that lives twelve ticks and then dies, and blowing something up is the same operation as a bird laying an egg: sub_9772 with a different type. That is the whole object model paying for itself." },
        { "id": "cmt_40uqxj", "address": "$1F40", "text": "CORRECTION to the comment above, from amber, and I accept it: the multiplexer EXISTS AND NEVER RUNS.\n\nMy reading of the mechanism at $925F is right — two banks of state, frame parity in $30, sub_929D for even frames and sub_92CF for odd — but it never fires, because nothing ever allocates a slot in 8-15.\n\nThe evidence is the zone data. sub_92F6 is the only allocator and every caller passes Y in 0..7; FindFreeObjectSlot searches 2 to $42-1, and $42 read out of all 42 zone records is 8 in forty-one of them and 6 in zone 6. $1F89-$1F8F are never written after the initial clear. So the test at $9283 always finds sprInUse zero, always takes the sub_929D path, and the shadow arrays at $1F48/$1F58/$1F68/$1F78 are written by nothing.\n\nSo this is machinery for sixteen sprites that the game as shipped drives with eight — which is a fact about the program, not about the analysis, and exactly the sort of thing only reading the data settles. Keep the field names: they are correct about what the code would do.\n\n(One consequence for anyone reading further: the frame-1 enable mask sprEnableMaskFrame1 at $1F99 stays whatever CheckOptionsKey and sub_92F6 left in it, and it is ORed into $D015 on odd frames regardless.)" },
        { "id": "cmt_fdgont", "address": "$6700", "text": "THE COMPLETE ZONE RECORD, 200 bytes. The first 152 are nineteen tables of eight - one row per attribute, one column per creature type - copied wholesale to $1DA0 by LoadZoneTemplates, so the offset here IS the offset from $1DA0 and every tmpl* label is a slice of this:\n\n  +$00 $1DA0 tmplSpawnInterval    ticks between spawns of this type\n  +$08 $1DA8 tmplMoveType         0 static, 1 linear, 2 accelerating (dispatch at $993A)\n  +$10 $1DB0 tmplAnimFirst        first sprite block of the animation\n  +$18 $1DB8 tmplAnimLast         last one; equal to the first means no animation\n  +$20 $1DC0 tmplAnimMode         low 6 bits are the frame delay, bits 6 and 7 select ping-pong\n  +$28 $1DC8 tmplTypeWhenShot     what it becomes when the bullet hits it\n  +$30 $1DD0 tmplTypeWhenExploded what it becomes when the camel hits it\n  +$38 $1DD8 tmplNextType         what it becomes when its lifetime runs out; $FF means die\n  +$40 $1DE0 tmplSpeedX           sign-magnitude, low 7 bits a countdown\n  +$48 $1DE8 tmplSpeedY           same\n  +$50 $1DF0 tmplField80          step size added to the position each move\n  +$58 $1DF8 tmplSpawnLimit       bit 7 counts against a per-zone cap, bit 6 and the low nibble select the extra behaviour at $9C64\n  +$60 $1E00 tmplSpawnX           spawn column, or 0 to mean random\n  +$68 $1E08 tmplSpawnY           spawn row, or 0 to mean random\n  +$70 $1E10 tmplSpawnMode        0 fixed, $10 random, anything else = place relative to a live object of type (low nibble - 1)\n  +$78 $1E18 tmplColour           sprite colour, bit 7 selects multicolour\n  +$80 $1E20 tmplLifetime         ticks before it turns into tmplNextType\n  +$88 $1E28 tmplScoreValue       low nibble = score digit column, high nibble = how many to add. $15 is 100, $56 is 50, $55 is 500. Zero means shooting it scores nothing and makes no sound\n  +$90 $1E30 tmplExplodeLength    how much neutronium an explosion of this type burns\n\nThen eight scalars read by $9CB5 in this order:\n  +$98 typeMulticolourMask, +$99 typeYExpandMask, +$9A typeXExpandMask,\n  +$9B the object slot limit (8 in 41 zones, 6 in zone 6),\n  +$9C the sprite bank index (only ever 0, 2, 4 or 6),\n  +$9D $D025, +$9E $D026, +$9F read and discarded.\n\nAnd finally +$A0..+$C7, the 40-character zone name in screen codes.\n\nWorked example, zone 0 type 7: interval $A1, move type 2, shape $B8 to $B8, spawn mode 7 - so it is placed on top of a live type 6 - score $55 = 500 points, and it turns into type 0 when shot. Type 0 in the same zone scores nothing, so the wave is a chain: the thing worth 500 becomes something worth nothing." },
        { "id": "cmt_xwg2xm", "address": "$4000", "text": "Confirmed multicolour, and it is the zone 30-35 set. Rendered at two bits per pixel — 12 pixels wide instead of 24, four colours — the shapes resolve cleanly: a fish or rocket, a tall box, a diagonal beam. Rendered as hires the same bytes come out dithered #.#. all over, which is the giveaway.\n\nThe zone records agree: $D025 and $D026, the two shared sprite multicolour registers, are set per zone from offsets +157 and +158, and every zone using this bank sets them to a real pair. The box is plausibly the portaloo of zone 31, SIDNEY AND THE PORTALAVS." },
        { "id": "cmt_yhh0ac", "address": "$5E00", "text": "THE IMAGE ALREADY CONTAINS A BUILT TABLE, and comparing it with the default proves how it got there.\n\nAll 208 bytes here are exactly defaultHighScoreTable at $5474 with each byte ANDed with $3F: 119 bytes identical (spaces and digits, which are the same in PETSCII and screen codes) and the other 89 differing by precisely that mask, none differing any other way. That is what InitDefaultHighScores at $545C does when it copies.\n\nSo this is not fresh RAM and it is not what LOAD put here. It is the result of $545C having been RUN on the machine the build was saved from - the same story as the assembler source at $5870 and $5F28. The shipped image is a memory dump of a development session, not a clean link.\n\nStructure, from the default: four entries of 52 bytes. \"n  0000000    \" is 14 characters - rank, two spaces, seven score digits, four spaces - then 38 characters of name. 4 x 52 = 208, which is exactly the $D0 that $C082 saves and $C065 loads.\n\nThe names are Jeff Minter placeholder jokes: I LIKE CAMELS REALLY, I GOT NOTHING AGAINST SHEEP EITHER, LLAMAS ARE LLOVELY, 2D SPACE INVADERS RULE THE ZONE OK. They are stored in the code as PETSCII and on screen as screen codes, which is why the file on disk holds screen codes and would look like control characters to anything else reading it." },
        { "id": "cmt_x6w0s0", "address": "$8A62", "text": "NEW GAME INIT - and this is where every starting value in the game is set. Reached from $8A3C after the screen row table is built.\n\nClears about twenty zero page bytes to zero, blanks the border and background, then:\n  $1F = 4     the fine scroll offset\n  $4A = $12   EIGHTEEN units of neutronium - the full bar\n  $4B = 3     drain sub-tick\n  $4C = 6     top of the neutronium colour ramp\n  $5A = 3     attract scroll state\n  $3F = $67   high byte of the zone data pointer, so it starts at $6700 = zone 0\n  $57 = 7     zone distance, the default the options screen edits\n  $54 = 5     FIVE BEASTS - the starting lives\n  $40 = 8     camel colour\n  $5D = $5A, $59 = 0, $5C = 0\n\nThen InstallIrqAndVicMode takes the machine over, the bottom five rows ($0720-$07E7) are filled with character $28 in colour 6 - the ground - and the VIC gets multicolour text, background colours and $D018 = $18 for screen $0400 / characters $2000.\n\nSo: 5 beasts, 18 neutronium, zone 0, distance 7. Everything else the player sees is derived from the zone record." },
        { "id": "cmt_647k5n", "address": "$9406", "text": "Blank the message line: 40 spaces over $0590-$05B7, then fall into ShowCheatModeBanner, which redraws the cheat banner if the cheat is on. Eight callers - anything that finishes with the message line comes through here, and the fall-through rather than a JSR is why the banner survives every clear." },
        { "id": "cmt_8b1ijy", "address": "$9413", "text": "Drag every object with bit 7 of objMoveType set one column to the left, stopping at X = $10. That bit is the with-the-scroll flag: objects that belong to the terrain move with it, objects that fly do not. Called once every other frame from the raster work phase at $8C20, which is the same cadence the terrain scrolls at." },
        { "id": "cmt_12c4rj", "address": "$942F", "text": "Push all sixteen object positions into the sprite shadow registers through WriteSpritePosition. Called first thing in CommitSprites, so the shadow registers are always one full pass behind the object updates - which is what stops a sprite being torn between its old X and its new Y." },
        { "id": "cmt_ii3ran", "address": "$6000", "text": "THE TUNE, measured rather than guessed. Summing the duration byte of every pair up to the $FF terminator:\n\n  voice 1  $6000-$6231  281 pairs  636 ticks  23 distinct notes, indices 33-64\n  voice 2  $6320-$64B3  202 pairs  636 ticks  19 distinct notes, indices 33-63\n  voice 3  $65A0-$66F5  171 pairs  604 ticks  15 distinct notes, indices 19-43\n\nVoices 1 and 2 come to EXACTLY the same total, which is what a three-part arrangement of one piece looks like and would not happen by accident. Voice 3 is 604 and then rests, so it is the bass line and it stops before the end. The first 38 pairs of all three voices are byte-identical to voice 1, so the piece opens in unison and splits.\n\nSpeed: the player runs once per call of CommitSprites, which on the game path is once a frame, and the tempo divider $10/$11 is 7. So a tick is about 0.14 s and the whole tune is around 90 seconds.\n\nThe note table it indexes is a true equal-tempered scale - adjacent entries are in ratio 1.0595 and index 12 is exactly twice index 0, 536 against 1072 - but it is not tuned to concert pitch. Index 0 is 536 * 985248 / 16777216 = 31.5 Hz, which sits between B0 at 30.87 and C1 at 32.70, so the whole table is about a third of a semitone sharp of B or two thirds flat of C. Calling any note here by a letter name means choosing which." },
        { "id": "cmt_316f2p", "address": "$9565", "text": "Four sprite shapes for the landing squash, CE CF CF CE, indexed 1-based by jumpVelocity counting 4 down to 1. The base operand is $9564, which is the RTS of the routine above - the same one-below-the-table trick the star seed tables use at $8A0D." }
      ],
